This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=987&MId=16256', '2026-09-17', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '16256', '', '2026-09-17', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.413523', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6f4f8b2734c4acb153e7', 'calendar', 'Senedd diary (senedd.tv)', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee — 23 September 2026, 09.30', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee meets on 23 September 2026 at 09.30 in Committee Room 1.
Agenda:
2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=978&MId=16233', '2026-09-23', '', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 'General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'https://www.senedd.tv/Meeting/Index/12aaf30b-1a56-4204-98cb-d7f0902b3846', '2026-09-23', 24.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-23T11:57:42.599906', 'seneddtv:12aaf30b-1a56-4204-98cb-d7f0902b3846', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6f7bc525490392c7023a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Trefnydd, Chief Whip and Cabinet Minister for Culture and Sport', 'need space to perform. That''s why music venues like the Dragonffli in Pontypool are vital. They give music acts a stage to grow their audience and hone their talents. They''ve also hosted many iconic acts there as well. The business rates cut announced yesterday will make a massive difference to music venues, but how can the Government, via Creative Wales, support both opportunities to nurture new talent and ensure that music venues across my constituency and Wales can continue to produce world-class musicians and bands?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Matthew Jones', '', '12151', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Trefnydd, Chief Whip and Cabinet Minister for Culture and Sport', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=300&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-09-17T11:56:12.530320', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769422', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6f8f1a0f03547212c6ab', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'In terms of these first 18 months, have you identified any issues that may prevent all these Bills coming through in this timeline at all?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Kiera Marshall', '', '12166', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=531&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.127592', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770365', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6f94c945c66e91343aa8', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Diolch, Minister. Following on from what our Reform spokesperson has already said, I would like to urge you further to have annual targets so that any progress on delivering those 20,000 social homes can be easily tracked. We all know that Rome wasn''t built in a day, but we would like to know how many homes will be built in Wales in a year.

 Minister, following alongside those aims to get people into social housing as soon as possible, I was wondering whether the pots of money currently given to registered social landlords to adapt old stock—current old stock—to meet the demand and needs of those on current waiting lists will continue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5635&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'registered social landlords; social homes; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.233884', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6fa911db60e714c56bdc', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Yes. Good morning, all. Thanks ever so much for joining us today. We''re undertaking this important bit of evidence around elections. I want to look first at evaluation of the pilots. We''d like to understand what the challenges were in delivering for you. How was it? What''s your view of what the pilots were telling you? There are going to be lots of connected questions, especially as we''re moving a lot closer to the next elections. Can you give us a flavour of what you found and what the challenges might be? Or was it good? You may not all want to answer the same question, I accept that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=3685&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.152315', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769910', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('6fbba1b0ba810d6c3907', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I agree and acknowledge that. Perhaps you could write to us, just setting out how you will seek to replicate, maybe, good examples with DEFRA more widely across the Welsh Government, so that then this committee can understand what the Welsh Government have done to secure that. Then, if there is an issue, we can more appropriately understand whether it was UK Government lack of engagement or a lack of engagement on the Welsh Government side. Would that be okay?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=3638&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.716273', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770455', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('708b06b6332f1f25b072', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Thank you, Cabinet Minister. The proposal is to agree the motion. Does any Member object? [ Objection .] There is objection. I will therefore defer voting under this item until voting time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=22026&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.435017', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767943', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('70949416f270e858d188', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'It''s following on from Sarah''s question, actually. The root causes are really important. As well as throwing money at a problem, it''s very important that we look at those, and it''s very important that we do provide those jobs and opportunities in Wales, on people''s doorsteps. I was just wondering how you''re working across parties to achieve those goals.

 But also, as well as directly and indirectly looking at this, have you looked at examples of best practices across the United Kingdom, in all parts, about what has worked in the past? And are you using that? Because I know it''s a tradition, it seems, in Wales that we sort of reject what they''re doing over the border, rather than just using that and Welshifying it and adapting it to what''s happening here. So, I hope that that''s the case. 

 Have we looked even further afield to that as well? Have you looked at other countries similar to ours, with a similar make-up, and seen what''s working there? I''d be interested to know how you''ve come to the conclusion about what you think is best in your new strategy, and how you''ve developed it. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1054&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.674117', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771203', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('70e3065fa599aa061514', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes. We seem to have walked into the cost-of-living area, and one thing that the National Farmers Union have been calling for is interest-free loans for the purchase of fertiliser, because, obviously, fertiliser, through the global consequences, has doubled in price and is likely to go up even higher. 

 The Government have a bank, which they use for business. Is the Government minded to try and make some financial facility available so that farmers can get interest-free loans to purchase fertiliser? Because, as a direct consequence of not having fertiliser, you reduce the output, and therefore you''re feeding a market, which is already hungry for product, at a lower rate, then, you are. And the numbers—. When you look at the farm balance sheet at the moment, with fuel through the roof, inflation creeping back in to Ukrainian levels—Ukrainian conflict levels—the balance sheet just really is unable to take these costs. So, a mechanism—which doesn''t cost the Government money; obviously, the loans have to be repaid—could be a way of working with the Government bank to create a facility for interest-free loans.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Andrew R.T. Davies', '', '143', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2519&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.658834', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772052', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('70eff07fe08d0996aa21', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thanks for that and thanks for pointing out the challenges and how they—. Well, can they be addressed? I have some concerns, and I think you have some significant concerns yourself around timing, yet you''re saying that it would be a shame if it doesn''t go ahead—that''s what I think you were saying—for these elections. But are you confident, then, that those areas of challenge can be sufficiently managed to enable this to actually happen for this next election? I''m sure there''ll be further questions on that later.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=395&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.734477', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769817', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('712bb047cc171409d8d8', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee

Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. The Committee’s forward work programme for the autumn term is now available. Please note that the programme is subject to change and will be reviewed and updated regularly to reflect the Committee’s ongoing work. Forward work programme: Autumn 2026 (PDF 80KB) ¬¬¬ Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee On the 9th July 2026 the Committee held its first meeting . During the meeting Members discussed options for determining a strategic approach to the work within their remit, including how to set out priority areas for scrutiny over the term. To help inform these discussions and to shape the Committee’s forward work programme, Members agreed to launch a consultation on priorities for the Seventh Senedd. The Committee is seeking views from people and organisations on the issues they believe it should prioritise in the Seventh Senedd. Further information on how to submit your views is available on the consultation page . Please share this consultation with anyone who you think would be interested in submitting their views. zzz Business type: Other First published: 11/06/2026 Documents Hide the documents Forward work programme: Autumn 2026 PDF 304 KB View as HTML (2) 54 KB Consultations Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee (running until 14/09/2026) Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', '2026-08-05', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 98.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; consultation; consultations; seeking views', 0, '2026-08-05T14:00:51.561947', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('714eecc5d7797af7d47b', 'committee_transcript', 'Petitions Committee', '2. Updates - Petitions referred to the Seventh Senedd', 'Option 2 is to refer the petition to the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee for scrutiny. If the petition is referred to another committee without any further correspondence with the Minister by the Petitions Committee, then this committee could then close its consideration of the petition. That wouldn''t be my choice. I don''t know how other people feel on this issue.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16175', '2026-07-09', 'Donna Cushing', '', '12168', '', '', 'Petitions Committee', '2. Updates - Petitions referred to the Seventh Senedd', '16175', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16175?startPos=401&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:13.216697', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16175&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('717b5495eb239d367811', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Again, as I said, it is the EHRC''s responsibility. The committee could have a view on how helpful or not that guidance is. I would suggest that that is something that the committee could explore, because I know there are concerns, but that isn''t a role for Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3540&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.965641', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771220', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('718af16707b144fd4b09', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', 'Since the dawn of devolution 27 years ago, the young people of Wales have been let down, both in terms of their education received and their attempts to find good employment, and the two are inextricably linked. It''s unforgiveable that a nation like ours with so much potential has become such an unattractive prospect for young people seeking employment, and that we are haemorrhaging precious home-grown talent in our youngsters and they are leaving Wales. But it''s little wonder. Wages here are lower and people pay a greater proportion of their income in tax than anywhere else in Britain. So, why would a young person, fresh out of education, yet to put down roots, not seek opportunities and settle elsewhere?

 The same stands for our businesses. Our young people—some of our young people—are the innovators of the future, the ones leaving education with fresh ideas for business start-ups. But with sky-high business rates and a poor infrastructure, transport wise, naturally, any business-savvy young person will look beyond Wales to minimise their costs and maximise their earnings.

 But even before our young people look at the job market, their choices have been limited by the worst performing education system in the UK. And I blame Welsh Labour and Plaid Cymru. They have far fewer opportunities than their counterparts over the border for courses, apprenticeships and degrees. Our infrastructure and transport networks play their part in this problem. They are suffering from a chronic lack of investment, and virtue-signalling policies like 20 mph and a ban on road building have damaged us and meant that business and jobs are not coming to Wales in anywhere near the numbers they should.

 I will say though that I very much welcome the commitment from the Welsh Government to a third Menai crossing in my own constituency of Bangor Conwy Môn, something that we have campaigned for for a long time. This will be critical to the expansion and new job opportunities at Wylfa, and that is exactly what we need to be replicating across Wales: investment in our transport infrastructure, building a much needed M4 relief road.

 Other harmful policies like the tourism tax. It just beggars belief why you keep hitting the very businesses, as Tom Montgomery actually put across quite well, that youngsters want to go into, in the hospitality industry. They want to work in a fish and chip shop or serve slushies at Woolworths. But we''re just doing everything we can to make it difficult for businesses to actually then encourage young people to go and work there.

 During the election, the Welsh Conservatives had a robust plan for economic growth. It really was the only credible plan to deliver more jobs and prosperity, and, critically, to bring us in line with the rest of the UK. Now, we are happy to work with Plaid Cymru, and I had a very constructive meeting with the Minister yesterday, Adam Price. We need to work together, as has been said, cross-party, but we need to be serious about using the levers that you have as a Welsh Government. You need to scrap business rates for small firms, pubs and post offices. We need to make public procurement contracts more accessible, particularly to our small business. We''ve been talking about this for 15 years, but it''s not happened. We must give priority to Welsh local firms.

 We need to work with education providers to expand the choice of courses available to young people, particularly in STEM subjects and apprenticeships. We need to take action to address mobile blackspots and poor broadband speeds, so that our young people in remote and rural communities aren''t left behind and their studies aren''t hindered. Crucially, we need to look at the problem with young people and unemployment in the round, starting with the improving of our education system, finishing with fixing our economy. That is the only way to create secure, well-paid jobs for our young people, to rival those of other UK nations and indeed the rest of the world.

 So, I agree with many of the really good points that have been made in this debate, but let''s not forget who is responsible for the mess we''re in with our job market. For Plaid, they''ve said all the right things about young people and employment before the election, but as I said on the day of my re-election, I will be really watching on this, because you will not grow Wales without growing your young people. I don''t feel very optimistic that you will deliver all you have promised, and we will hold you to account on it—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Janet Finch-Saunders', '', '212', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=10376&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', '', '', 'business rates; tourism tax', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.709663', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768285', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('72045043c14b4b533f05', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '''Ensure funders consider all options and choose the appropriate mechanism(s) that will effectively deliver agreed outcomes throughout the funding period.''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Elfed Williams', '', '12165', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6979&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.308696', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771271', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('721c1005acc159df7a52', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you, Minister. Two questions. Firstly, I wanted to ask not only how the targets are measurable—I think you''ve given us a lot on that—but what consideration have you given as to how these targets will tackle the underlying causes of poverty and are not just what we''ve heard previously of sticking plasters for short-term results? I''d also like to ask, on the Cynnal payments specifically, could you tell us a bit more about how the UK Government are co-operating on the roll-out of that Cynnal pilot? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sarah Rees', '', '5196', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=780&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.642508', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771206', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('724a05f0d439efea0690', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you. We''re about halfway through, so I was going to suggest a couple of minutes'' break, if that''s okay—happy, everyone? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3566&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.970247', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771222', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('728b2925350034cabada', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Thank you. In terms of going forward, do you think it''s possible to establish a common data point or a common data standard? So, you mentioned earlier UPRN, which probably wouldn''t work in Powys, but might in other local authorities, so that, again, the amount of work needed to be done to do that matching reduces in future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Elyn Stephens', '', '12164', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5664&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.464821', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769923', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7384df5941badae2ad05', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Correct. I have had no involvement in the process, and neither will I until, obviously, I receive their final report.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6246&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.136619', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772108', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('738b62ab38107dce525e', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I''ll use the Chair''s prerogative now to try and bring this part on wildfires to a close, with one final question from me, Cabinet Minister. I think, during the whole time when we had the crisis, a lot of people were very unsure as to where civil contingencies sat within the Welsh Government, who was responsible, which Ministers were responsible for what. In the previous Government, in your ministry, the Llywydd now, Huw Irranca-Davies, who was the rural affairs Minister, held civil contingencies. I think it was very unclear for a lot of us, as Members, and, obviously, public bodies as well, as to who was responsible in Government. So, is that something that Government has thought about since and how that can be streamlined, so we actually, from a Government perspective, know who is responsible, who will be in charge of mitigating these things in the future, rather than it being spread across so many different portfolios and then things falling through the gaps? Is that some learning that the Government is going to do, going forward?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2704&l=en', NULL, 63.2, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.688627', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771879', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('738c0b8b45eb11ce7a45', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thank you. Mike, did you want to ask a question around frequency of registration? [ Inaudible. ] Sorry. Mike, bear with me a second.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1599&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.931131', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769821', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('73a9efa87573ad48190d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Yes, absolutely. Will you commit to amending the allocation of housing and homelessness regulations, to restrict the eligibility and give preference to British applicants? And will you use your legal powers to set a standard compelling every registered social landlord in Wales to record and publish the nationality of applicants and tenants?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4183&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'homelessness; registered social landlord', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.090431', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768181', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('73e0c70d46dc294d3277', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', 'Well, it''s always been something that''s been a topic of debate in this Senedd, and within Plaid Cymru too, in terms of what is that point where you''re striking the balance in the housing market between the housing stock available for people to live in and also the housing stock available for the tourism and self-catering sector, and striking that balance, so that one doesn''t have a negative impact on the other, getting that balance right, is important. And that is what we hope to deliver through consulting on this.

 I''ve already had discussions with representatives of the self-catering sector, who, clearly, do believe that the balance is at a lower point than the 152 days in the consultation, and you perhaps feel the same. Now, I am open to having that discussion. I sense that the response to the consultation will demonstrate that the sector itself, and those within the sector, do believe that we need to go deeper than what''s contained within the consultation, but the Government does want to strike the right balance. We don''t want to create further problems for ourselves by setting this threshold too low. But I am eager that we reach a position where this system that impacts our housing sector, but also our self-catering and tourism sectors, reaches a point where it can be a permanent solution in this area. So, I''m open to discuss this with you too in terms of how we get the outcome to this consultation to reach a point that has majority support in this place.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=3513&l=en', NULL, 104.5, 'High', 'digest', 'second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; self-catering', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:16.994038', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769539', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('74022678fdc26fc70b24', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Wales needs more houses. You''ve said that today, Minister, but we heard in the press over the weekend developers and housing associations saying that it can take between three and five years to secure planning consent.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5144&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'housing associations; planning consent', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.186351', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768174', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7405490782a0e1ab0ef9', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I think there''s an enormous amount of work going into the development of the housing portfolio, of Bills and of policy in that area. The Member makes a very important point: that you can legislate as much as you like, but if you haven''t got the financial resource around that legislation to make sure that it is effective, then it won''t be effective legislation. So, those are discussions that are ongoing. I''m sure there''ll be discussions across parties on a number of those issues as they develop, and there is considerable work to be done in the field of housing.

 Some of that doesn''t necessarily require resource, of course. Some of it requires structural changes to the legislation rather than necessarily resource.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2156&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.438341', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770388', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('745c6eba864bcb1e93c6', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I might need to pick that up. One of the recommendations of the review was to have stronger leadership around the legislative programme. So, whilst some functions were already being fulfilled at the time, a deputy director position, which is the role that I''m in now, was created to bring that management and coherence to the programme as a whole. I might add that that has brought a specific focus on making sure that when we think about the legislative programme, we''re not just thinking about primary legislation, we''re also thinking about the subordinate legislation, going to the Member''s earlier question. There''s finite resource, and that package really needs to be choreographed very carefully to make sure that it can all be delivered.

 The Cabinet Minister mentioned the legislation pool as well. Just to give you a little bit more information around that, that''s a pool of individuals who essentially belong in my division, but that I can deploy out to the Bill projects that need them the most. That not only gives us quite an agile way into some of these projects, perhaps more agile than previously, but it also brings expertise within a cohort—people who have managed a Bill before, people who have experience in that very difficult job of taking a policy idea and working with lawyers, stakeholders and economists to build it into instructions to legislative counsel that can work and can be delivered successfully and to time. I just think it''s a good illustration of trying to build not just more capacity but more agility and more expertise into the system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Sophie Brighouse', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2523&l=en', NULL, 115.0, 'High', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Bill progress', 'Legislation Committee; legislative programme; subordinate legislation', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.514056', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770452', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('746e83b0d3c05051ab6e', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'As a result of that being raised yesterday, we''ll be writing out in the next few days to returning officers, chief executives, to say, ''Please can you place that emphasis?'' Often, electoral teams sit within the legal area of a council, the monitoring officer area of a council, which is often where data protection sits as well, so unlocking that co-ordination shouldn''t be that difficult; it just needs that emphasis and priority from the top of the organisation. But, again, I think that also brings home that this is a live issue, the importance of that working group co-ordinating and making sure it addresses the risks that you''re picking out today and mitigates those in time for May 2027.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5998&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; working group', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.519152', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769952', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7496ea058ef23364ac4d', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes. I''ll draw this bit of the session to a close, and we''ll move on to animal health and welfare, but my question on this, and it''s probably pertinent to what other Members have just asked, is that you are the ultimate Minister responsible for the climate change budget within Government, but a lot of the things that can mitigate climate change, the things we have to do to get towards a cooler planet, actually sit in multiple portfolios across Government. I know previous Governments used to have ministerial round-tables where they used to come together to discuss these matters, as you well know, because you sat in this chair before. I''m interested in how this Government is approaching that and how you bring all those portfolios together to support our move to a cooler planet, because, if we don''t, individual portfolios of Government will go on and do their own thing and work in silos. I''m interested, from your perspective, as the ultimate portfolio holder, in how you''re bringing all that together to deliver on some of the aims that the Government and the Senedd have set us for Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=5107&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.981719', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771851', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('74b5bf64ed235e79c25f', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Yes, so that''s going to be determined by each registration officer independently, based on their own assessment of the availability of the data and whether it is suitable for the matching purpose that they need to use. As you mentioned, council tax is absolutely the largest data set that''s available to all registration officers, and it''s also the data set that, from the pilot schemes, identified the largest number of potential electors. But then there will be a number of different data sets, and we know that in the pilots lots of the authorities used education data, as that''s the data set that will allow the simplest additions to the register using only one data source. And it also helps because we know that younger voters are the ones who are least likely to be registered.

 So, it''s going to be down to each individual registration officer to make that assessment. They''ll need to make sure they have a really clear view about how the data was compiled, what sorts of verification processes were in place when that data was being compiled, what coverage it''s likely to have, whether it''s universal or it''s targeting particular groups, how frequently that data is being updated, so is it older data or has it been refreshed relatively recently? Those are all considerations that they''re going to need to take into account.

 We''ve flagged guidance about this. We''ve been able to publish guidance for registration officers this week, which is based on the regulations as they''ve been tabled. So, it''s guidance about what they might do once the regulations come into force. And that sets out some of the questions that registration officers will need to be asking themselves to help satisfy themselves that they''re getting hold of the right data.

 I think just one other concern that we know registration officers have been flagging is whether they can actually secure access to data, or even where it''s held within their local authority. Some of them have reported maybe some challenges in getting access to that data, and it does take time sometimes to achieve that. So, it might be that, again, for this first year, there are data sources that haven''t been made available in time for the matching process. But in future years, with more time available, that might be more possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2350&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.054592', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769882', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('74ea2f41f0b56eb0f109', 'committee_transcript', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. General scrutiny session with the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care, the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health and the Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health', 'That is a very good point, and yes, that is going to be part of our consideration. That''s why I talk about looking at a whole-year programme, rather than just looking at three months out of the year. And to expand on that, I have asked them to consider, for example, El Niño, because we know that the strongest El Niño ever is going to strike us; it''s already at work across the world now. But there are potential consequences to El Niño that, of course, will affect our community and the health and care services. So, we need to consider the consequences of that when it comes to, for example, more rain and flooding, damp in people''s homes. Are people going to suffer more from respiratory problems because of these things? Are we going to see hospitals suffering because of the fact that there is flooding, or is there anything like that that could affect us? Those new weather patterns do need to be part of our considerations.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16251', '2026-09-17', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. General scrutiny session with the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care, the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health and the Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health', '16251', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16251?startPos=2909&l=en', NULL, 40.2, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'hmos_and_standards', 'Private rented sector', '', '', 'damp', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:14.303945', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16251&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770109', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7503b8cfe4622310f3d4', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Kiera, did you want to—?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=984&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.205712', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770408', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('75077fef986e3dcddeb4', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Thank you. Peter, you''ve got a question.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6473&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.611415', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769978', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('75262d7462b8935cfe48', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', 'The points you''ve made and the illustrations you''ve given as to how important the hospitality sector is in our various communities, and how important it is for different aspects of the economy, how many of our young people have their first jobs in pubs—. I had my first job in a pub, and I pulled my last pint in a pub in 1987. So, I hadn''t done it until I pulled a pint just recently, and it showed that 30 years and more had passed since I pulled a pint.

 But to be serious and to respond to the points that you''ve made, this will be a contribution that alleviates some of the pressure on our hospitality and leisure sector. It is more than just pubs, it is cafes and restaurants, and it is gyms and museums and libraries. So, it''s the widest possible array, and when you add that to shops as well, it''s the widest possible array of many of the businesses that feature in our town centres and some of our villages as well. So, it goes further than Government has done before in Wales. So, that''s the important contribution of today. But I''m sure, as some have said already, that there are a myriad of different other issues that face our hospitality and leisure sectors. There are many pressures in our economy. The inflationary pressure that we are feeling today and will possibly be increasing again is something that puts particular pressure on so many of our high-street businesses. There are actions that the UK Government as well as the Welsh Government can take. But, hopefully, today will have been a strong signal that this Government and this Senedd believe that our small businesses that are trying to work so hard to keep going in our communities right throughout Wales in difficult times—this Government has their back and will try and take all initiatives that it can to support them in these testing times.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=11250&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.265363', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769234', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7583d718346e1c9210ac', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Sorry, I missed the last part of your question.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4341&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.994483', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771297', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('758d7a66e92dd4f191ed', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '1. Motion to elect a temporary Chair under Standing Order 17.22 for this meeting', 'May I propose Peredur Owen Griffiths, please?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '1. Motion to elect a temporary Chair under Standing Order 17.22 for this meeting', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=36&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.041670', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770418', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('759a89659ba3c43f0e5b', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Quickly on that, I''d just like to know if there''s any consideration of the attendance of Ministers and the effectiveness of TSPCs. And maybe it''s something you want to come back to in future, Minister, but I''d like to ask whether there''s been any assessment of the previous Welsh Government strategy on volunteering, and whether you''ve considered any link between the increased pressures in terms of the cost of living and the decrease in volunteering. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sarah Rees', '', '5196', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=7523&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.363685', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771314', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('763819bf9bc7af297244', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'During our meeting with the previous Chair, it was raised very firmly that past experience has not been very good in terms of what you''ve just said about the backlog suddenly coming along and inundating the committee with work, which possibly couldn''t be done. And so we are mindful of that and we are concentrating on the timing, as my colleague says. The timing is vitally important to us, but it''s crucial for you if you''re going to do your work properly, which you—. And if this committee is unable to scrutinise, then the whole thing falls down. So, we are mindful of what you say and we are mindful of the need not to overload this committee, but there is one caveat—and this is just repeating what my colleague said—and that is that we cannot control what comes from Westminster. With the best will in the world, that can''t be done. But, other than that, all the other matters within our control, we will deal with in a manner compatible with the best interests of your committee.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1112&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.229729', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770355', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('76517abeff94e11eeeb0', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch, Gadeirydd. Community cohesion is something that''s all our responsibility, but, obviously, Government has to take a lead as well. The previous Government did seek to strengthen community cohesion through its community cohesion programme. I know that you, in your priority statement, have committed to progressing the recent community cohesion expert group''s recommendations. So, I''m just wondering if you could tell us a little bit more about that work, how it''s likely to be measured, and how, as a committee, we can keep up to date with the work of the recommendations group.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jayne Bryant', '', '5038', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5884&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.174024', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771303', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7678163508fce03fa049', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Mike Connolly, deputy director of the community safety division. Good morning.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Mike Conolly', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=89&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.560415', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771142', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('76b8395788b2bd4535d5', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'And this was something that came through very strongly in the Cabinet when we were discussing this. Of course, my role is to ensure that that awareness, understanding and responsibility are shared. So, that, for instance, those opportunities through relationships and sexuality education, was essentially important and core to that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5856&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.167354', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771296', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('76fc5e4371a029c8ace1', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Delivering meaningful change for people in Pen-y-bont Bro Morgannwg, and across Wales, is at the heart of this Government''s approach. That starts with our first 100 days plan, on which I''m working collaboratively with Cabinet colleagues to deliver. The programme for government will build on this foundation, with improved cross-Government working and a clear focus on delivery.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=4705&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.620795', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767664', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7780fe7ce26bbf8f8d15', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you very much. I just have one other brief question, if that''s okay. You talked about the steps and the milestones, in your mind, do you have an idea of when we''ll be in a situation when we will know what those look like? And I hope I know the answer to this: are you in touch with other Governments, such as the Scottish Government—and we have also heard from Finland and other places—and are you looking at what they''ve done and how to keep an eye on countries such as ours, which are small, for example?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=633&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.620787', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771149', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('77914157454d093dd6a4', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thank you. I''ve got some other questions, but I''m going to write to you, if that''s okay, with the questions on future evaluation of AVR. Obviously, I''ll share the questions with the committee members, and the subsequent response. But right now, I''d just like to thank you for giving evidence and providing us with a bit more clarity around the process. We will send you the minutes, if you like, of the meeting this morning, just to ensure that we''ve captured the accuracy of what we''ve been discussing. But, again, many thanks from the committee.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2819&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.124399', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769933', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('77f41be79d0510187a41', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Yes. Thank you. I''m now unmuted. I certainly do. Can you expand on the rationale for reducing the required number of automatic voter registration exercises from four to one and the potential effect on areas with higher levels of mobility—i.e. areas like the ones I represent, with lots of houses in multiple occupation and lots of students, where you have substantial movement during the year?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1608&l=en', NULL, 138.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; houses in multiple occupation; multiple occupation', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.934744', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769834', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('783c592316ddd61fef1f', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I''m Dafydd Trystan Davies and I''m the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution. So, as a team from Government legal services, we are very pleased to be here, Chair.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', 'Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=203&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.060862', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770348', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('789e79ab383e1b829c71', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Obviously, as I was saying, I will be taking— . The Cabinet will soon be committing, as a Government—. We''ll be discussing this over the next few weeks for that exact reason. We, as a Government, need to define our relationship with the third sector. Of course, I do engage every day with members of the third sector, and with organisations such as the WCVA and so on. So, as a Minister, I am fully aware and have stated on the record that I acknowledge the importance of this sector, and so I would hope to make sure that that relationship is an effective one. I don''t know if there''s anything else specific that needs to be added on engagement with the third sector.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=7439&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.350775', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771298', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('78ba24d43720e9a853cd', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '[ Inaudible. ]—of Aberystwyth University, I fully support what he said.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4685&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.921329', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770605', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7908c036acae24a4b8fd', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Minister, this is a minority Government and, over the last few weeks, this Senedd has demonstrated that Plaid cannot simply assume that others will just fall into line and vote with them. If the Government wants to secure the support of this Chamber, it must be prepared to build the consensus across political divides and to listen to those who represent different viewpoints. Every one of us in this Chamber has been elected to represent our constituents and to honour the mandate they have given us. Our responsibility is not to make life easier for the Government, but to ensure that the voices of the people we represent are heard in this place.

 In Reform, our position has been clear from the outset. We have three simple red lines to ensure that we support a budget that actually puts Wales first. I''d like to first say that none of those red lines is directly copying what England are doing with the ALN funding, so I look forward to her apology for the comments she initially made regarding Reform''s position.

 The first red line is we called for international spending to be brought to an end, so that those millions of pounds could instead be invested here in Wales, as they always should have been. The Welsh Government budget was always intended to be spent in Wales.

 Secondly, we called for greater investment in our home-grown NHS staff. It is unacceptable that newly qualified Welsh nurses are completing their training only to find there are too few opportunities for them to work in the NHS here in Wales. We invested in training these professionals, yet this year, nearly 400 nurses and midwives could not find jobs in the NHS after initial job allocations. Coincidentally, the Welsh Government has hired at least 500 nurses and other medical professionals from India through the Wales in India initiative.

 Thirdly, we highlighted, as other parties have, the urgent need for greater support for the additional learning needs system. There is no excuse for not being aware of this strain, even before we all highlighted it to you. Concerns have been raised about these financial pressures from Audit Wales to local councils to school leaders.

 Unfortunately, on the first two priorities, the Government has failed to act. On additional learning needs, while the Government has acknowledged the issue, what has been offered to local councils is only a fraction of what is required for our schools. It falls well short of the level of support that school leaders have been calling for. It is clear that rather than attempting to fix the system, this is an offer designed to secure political support, treating disabled children like a political football, and I know my colleague will touch on this later.

 Llywydd, at the election, Reform became the largest opposition group in the history of the Senedd. That was not an accident. It reflected the desire of many people across Wales for a different approach and for different voices to be heard in Welsh politics. We were not elected to wave through Government budgets that, in our view, fail to deliver for the people of Wales. We were elected to hold the Government to account and to argue for policies that reflect the mandate we have received. The people who sent us here expect us to stand by our principles, and that is exactly what we''re doing today. The majority of people in Wales did not vote for Plaid''s manifesto, and the majority of the Members of the Senedd were not elected on its programme. The proportional representation system that Plaid helped to design was always going to result in a minority Government. You knew you needed to seek collaboration.

 From the very first sitting in this Senedd, Reform has made it clear that we are prepared to work constructively with Members from across the political spectrum where proposals genuinely benefit the people of Wales. That offer still remains on the table, but that offer requires collaboration, not the expectation that an opposition party will sheepishly support a budget that fails to deliver for their voters. And for these reasons, Llywydd, Reform will be voting against the supplementary budget.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Cai Parry-Jones', '', '12174', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23691&l=en', NULL, 33.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Delivery bodies', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.554714', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767966', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7994a3b9053db8a77ddf', 'legislation', 'Welsh Statutory Instruments 2025', 'The Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025 (Commencement) Order 2025', 'The Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025 (Commencement) Order 2025', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2025/1305/made/data.xml', '2025-12-16', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 84.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', '', 'Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy); visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:15.224560', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/wsi/2025/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('79a0743dce6839c20602', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Diolch. So, I completely agree that prevention is better than cure. Keep it out, that is the way forward, and there are different tools and technology. I think a crucial piece here, and it also speaks to the question you raised, Chair, earlier, is about vets and farmers working closely together to look at the individual herd and flock and the health status and the diseases and conditions of concern, whether those are conditions that are already present in a herd or flock or those that might be coming towards. And to look at that, with vet and farmer in partnership, to assess, on the one hand, biosecurity, as you''re rightly asking, John, keeping disease out, prevention, and using different tools to assist that conversation, but also then to take action to ensure that that health planning, if you will—that animal health and welfare planning—translates into an improvement cycle. And, going back to your specific point, James, that is the animal health improvement cycle in universal action 12 of the sustainable farming scheme in a nutshell. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Richard Irvine', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6776&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.224803', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772120', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('79ba49e3ee25e7d1e0e8', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you very much. This Government, as you know, has ambitions in terms of the devolution of a number of powers over welfare. So, I have already, through correspondence and also through meetings, had several meetings with Ministers in the UK Government, namely the Secretary of State for Work and Pensions and also the Parliamentary Under-Secretary of State (Minister for Transformation). So, I''ve set out, in those meetings, our ambitions in terms of receiving additional powers over devolution, so that we can create a system that is more fit for purpose in terms of the kind of support that we want to provide for our families and communities here. So, of course, ensuring that we can be enabled to pilot Cynnal is part of those discussions. That is part of our powers under the Government of Wales Act 2006, but, of course, as I mentioned earlier, we do need support to do that in terms of the Treasury, to ensure that no family or child that receives those payments is worse off as a result.

 So, I do think that there is more that could be done with regard to the welfare system. I have thought, for example, in the short term that there is more that could be done in that regard, in terms of ensuring that we can share data better, given that the DWP isn''t always able to share the data, or isn''t willing to share the data, that we need in order to use those data to ensure that what we provide under our powers does reach the pockets of people as quickly and as easily as possible—those people who are eligible for that support.

 So, I have emphasised that Wales needs to have a voice in these decisions, and that Wales''s priorities need to be reflected in full when the decisions are made. I''ve made that clear, for example, with Sir Stephen Timms, who, of course, as the committee is probably aware, is undertaking a review at present of the personal independence payments. We know that any change in that regard is going to have a disproportionate impact on Wales because of the number of people in Wales who receive those PIP payments, and disabled people. So, I have said clearly to that Minister that we need to ensure that the voice of Wales and the voice of disabled people in Wales who will be impacted by these reforms are heard more clearly. There has been a great deal of discontent expressed in that regard, and I support that discontent, and have expressed that.

 So, I do look forward to having that positive and productive dialogue with the UK Government on this, because it is a crucially important agenda. It''s something that has so much influence on the lives and the quality of life of people in Wales. So, I do think that we need to ensure that our stance is clear, that our voice is heard and that we work together, whilst urging the devolution of these powers, and that we ensure that we can do everything we can, such as being able to share data with the DWP, to ensure that what we provide can reach those families as easily and as quickly as possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2105&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.799392', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771192', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('79db2ed167a74718e0f4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Small businesses are the backbone of our local economy in Bangor Conwy Môn. A clear example of this in Bangor is Antoniazzi''s cafe, which has been a prominent presence on the high street for 92 years now. In recent conversations with a number of businesses recently, the same request often rose, namely a fair deal on business rates, and I look forward to hearing the finance Minister''s statement later on this afternoon about that. But does the First Minister agree with me', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Mair Rowlands', '', '5084', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=571&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-09-16T11:53:07.097271', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768989', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('79feda279b333a968beb', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Just in terms of an update on the work, the expert group reported in March, published its recommendations, so we''re currently considering those recommendations as part of the ongoing work with the Deputy First Minister, and we''ll be making, I''m sure, a response to that report in due course. But, over the course of that expert group, we also worked with the expert group to define and set out a set of cohesion principles, which are on the website, and that describes what cohesion looks like in Wales and how these principles would help organisations and Government understand what it looks like. So, that work is in train. There are a number of recommendations that have already been delivered, but there''s work to be done on what the full response is.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Andrew Charles', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6093&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.193950', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771248', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7a01af04c06f279a264b', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '[ Inaudible .]—but I think also I mentioned earlier about looking at the legislation in terms of the regulations, in the Representation of the People (England and Wales) Regulations 2001, which are used as the basis for accessing information, and reflecting what UK Government has done to help remove those barriers, and also looking at the nature of some data sets as they are formed from national data sets—so, housing benefit would use DWP data—and then that''s acted as a barrier as well to obtaining a really good data source potentially that could be used for that. So, again, just looking at the framework that sits around the legislation to allow greater access as well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6042&l=en', NULL, 92.0, 'High', 'digest', 'welfare_and_support; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; housing benefit', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.526823', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769954', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7a6b498ea31acae028fd', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'So, item 3 this morning is the general scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution. It''s good to see you here. It''s good to see that you found your way to this meeting room. It is nice to have you here. Would you be able to introduce yourselves and your staff and officials for the record, please? I will begin with Elfyn. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=139&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.048196', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770345', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7a6b94299c9087eefc3f', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yeah, I take that point and I agree with it. As I was saying earlier, many of the powers that we could use to regulate how this misinformation is spread are not available to us. But, as I said, I do think that there are things that we can do to make communities more resilient to be able to withstand that misinformation. Obviously, we work very closely with partners who see the impact of this through the third sector and the voluntary sector, who could share with us their ideas and ensure that we are able to support them in the work that they''re doing in terms of trying to tackle this. In terms of the new agency, I would certainly be interested to engage with that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6197&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.207934', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771263', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7a96e1eb50cc7275de0a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'It might well have been a priority for the last Government. I doubt whether it''s a priority for your party right now because you voted against the supplementary budget, which offered £145 million to develop diagnostic hubs and elective care hubs across Wales. But, yes, it is a priority.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mabon ap Gwynfor', '', '269', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=1651&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.845058', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768058', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7b265adf563719e143e0', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'May I make one comment?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=5240&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:10.044365', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770565', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7b2d87a63a7ebdf4e6a3', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Thank you, Chair. Yes, my question is specifically on carbon budget 3. As we know, it''s quite a big step, but, obviously, a really important step as well. I just wanted to touch on the fact that, if I''m right from what I''ve been reading, 60 per cent of the emissions reductions required come from policy areas that are reserved or partially devolved. So, I was just wondering what your relationship is looking like with the UK Government, and are you getting those kind of assurances that you need from them as well that the policies and actions needed in those areas—along with, obviously, actions from the Welsh Government as well—will be in place to ensure that we meet our goals with that budget?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Niamh Salkeld', '', '12143', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4286&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.846448', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771793', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7b7669849680b34b263a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'The Cynnal payment has the potential to make a real difference to families facing the sharpest end of child poverty, particularly in communities like Afan Ogwr Rhondda, where levels of deprivation are among the highest in Wales and household budgets are already under enormous pressure. But, for it to work, families need clarity and confidence that this support will reach them directly. We know there are clear precedents for additional payments being disregarded within the benefits system, including the Scottish child payment and local welfare provision. But the Welsh NHS and social care bonus show that without Department for Work and Pensions protection, the value of a Government payment can be reduced through the universal credit system as it''s then classed as earnings. If the DWP is unwilling to provide that protection for an anti-poverty payment aimed at children, does the Dirprwy Brif Weinidog agree that this underlines exactly why decisions affecting poverty in Cymru should be made in Cymru by a Government that understands and prioritises communities in Cymru? And will she set out what discussions the Government is having to ensure that the Cynnal payment is simple to access and protected in full?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Elyn Stephens', '', '12164', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=710&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; LGHP Committee members', '', 'universal credit', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.519129', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766807', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7c7ddc97e563a84df84e', 'oral_question', 'Question not reached in Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'What assessment has the Cabinet Minister made of the impact of housing and planning policy on the ability of local authorities to deliver enough affordable homes for local people in Flintshire and Wrexham?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Nigel Williams', '', '5113', '', '', 'Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:06.987962', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=QNR', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7c9544cf12a411e4b1be', 'legislation', 'Acts of Senedd Cymru', 'Planning (Consequential Provisions) (Wales) Act 2026', 'Planning (Consequential Provisions) (Wales) Act 2026', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/2026/7/2026-04-28/data.xml', '2026-07-23', '', '', '', '', '', 'legislation.gov.uk', '', '', '', NULL, 39.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'named_welsh_legislation', 'Context', '', '', 'Planning (Consequential Provisions) (Wales) Act', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:14.610067', 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/data.feed', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7cb5aab632b9d3b74fb9', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Mike, your comments are noted. So, that agreement—[ Interruption .]', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24358&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.629926', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767896', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7cb6735fa29c0d66d3b4', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I''m assuming that you''re going to continue with the review that was taken on. I''m wondering, as part of that review, whether there has been a lot of movement in staff. Has there been redeployment to make sure that we are aligning your capacity to what you might be wanting to deliver, as a result of some of what was found in the review?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2492&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.505129', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770449', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7cbc1e982314e6ceec9b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you for that answer; it was longer than I expected. We have agreed there is an increasing demand for certain council services. There are also environmental health and trading standards—vital services that need to be supported. Things like food safety and trading standards protect consumers. Minister, how much extra money have you and fellow Ministers asked for from the finance Minister to meet this increased demand out of the £247 million unallocated?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4371&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'local_government_enforcement', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'environmental health', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.110956', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768149', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7d1f6b1035aa169649db', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Having come from Merthyr, which is the smallest authority involved, I see they''re already on a staff of bare bones. They can''t get people in because they can''t afford to increase their—. In fact, they’re actually getting rid of people. So to bring people into you, and to use other teams to do the data—it''s not possible. I can see in Merthyr it wouldn''t be possible, to be honest with you. So, I feel—and thank you; you have painted a picture for me—that 2027 is a bit too far, to be honest with you. I think we''re going a bit fast. I think that we put a lot on councils who are already stretched and we''re expecting them to take another challenge yet again when they’re already too stretched to do the work that they''re there to do now. So, that''s my point there.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'David Hughes', '', '12159', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6748&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.659491', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770318', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7d7e337b884f42042e5c', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. Unfortunately, I can''t comment on specific developments in specific constituencies. Welsh Ministers are responsible for making decisions in those areas, and who knows with this particular issue, so I can''t talk about that specifically. But you do bring forward an important point about how our planning policies and our strategies look at issues relating to data centres, which bring us back to the point made by James Evans, namely the need to look again at the main national development framework, ''Future Wales''. We do need to reform that. This is one example of why, and so that work is happening. During our first 100 days, there will be a specific announcement about the process that needs to happen with that. There is a specific process; we can''t just sit down and change it. We have to go through a process, and we will be making an announcement about that during the first 100 days.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=5065&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister; Other committee chairs', '', 'Future Wales', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.180802', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768175', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7dc0a578cec311ab5309', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '—but this is very important too. So, just a couple of minutes, perhaps another five minutes, if that''s okay.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6799&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.278548', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771254', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7e65c5e3fdf0b70a5e53', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', 'Thank you, Llywydd. Today, I''m pleased to announce changes the Welsh Government will make to reflect our manifesto commitments to rebalance the non-domestic rates system, and level the playing field for high-street businesses. We recognise the importance of vibrant retail, hospitality and leisure sectors, and these are central to our ambition to regenerate town centres and to breathe new life into our high streets.

 Our work of rebalancing the rates system will be delivered through changes to the existing differential multipliers, improving support for selected hospitality and leisure businesses. An expanded scope for the lower multiplier will be funded by a marginal increase to the higher multiplier, applicable to the highest value properties in the tax base across all sectors.

 From 2027-28, selected small to medium-sized hospitality and leisure properties will be subject to the lower multiplier currently applied to retail shops. This will bring eligible hospitality and leisure businesses in line with the permanent support introduced for retailers from this year. Eligible properties with rateable values below £51,000 will attract a 30 per cent reduction in their non-domestic rates bills.

 This permanent support will replace and exceed the temporary discretionary relief of 15 per cent for food and drink hospitality properties that is currently being provided. It will mean that a wider range of hospitality and leisure businesses will get the long-term certainty that they need to invest and to grow.

 The eligible food and drink hospitality properties are pubs, restaurants, cafes, bars, food courts, licensed clubs and live music venues. The eligible visitor accommodation properties are hotels, guest houses and hostels. And in the leisure sector, the eligible properties are cinemas, theatres, libraries, museums and gyms.

 These businesses play important and complementary roles in vibrant high streets and town centres. Along with retailers, ratepayers for around 20,000 properties will therefore benefit from the expanded lower multiplier. Over 12,500 more properties of the relevant types already attract full relief, and that means that they therefore do not pay any non-domestic rates. Ratepayers for a majority of properties across Wales, particularly small businesses, will also continue to benefit from a range of permanent reliefs currently worth more than £200 million per year.

 There is a balance to strike between the broader support that is provided through the lower multiplier and the resulting impact on the value of the higher multiplier. The higher multiplier will be set at just over 4 per cent above the standard multiplier from 2027-28 onwards. This is a small increase to the existing difference, and it''s a fair way to redistribute liability so that we can better support the businesses that are at the heart of our high streets and town centres.

 These changes to the differential multipliers are linked with the broader work of the town centres taskforce. This taskforce will take account of national policy frameworks, as well as considering the more tailored support that may be needed in individual town centres. Our approach recognises that needs will vary between places that may be facing different challenges that might go beyond the rate system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', 'Cabinet Minister for Finance', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=9363&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates; visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:25.991364', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769211', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7ec2dfcf97a715df300f', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you. Sarah Rees.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2347&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.820562', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771166', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7f683f8276a6e29080d7', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Well, I reject the suggestion that this Government discriminates according to someone''s ethnic background. What we are doing, and what the equality action plans that fall within my portfolio and responsibility as a Minister, which then run across the Government, for instance into education, do is to ensure that those who are facing the greatest obstacles in their lives are supported so that we do ensure that the disadvantage that people experience—and we have a great deal of evidence of that, which I can provide to the committee if some Members are not aware of that evidence—that we ensure that those people receive the support that they need in order to make things equal for them.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4242&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.982491', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771239', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7f7d8a182a006c05d1af', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill', 'Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill

Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill Legislative Consent: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill (the Bill) was introduced in the House of Commons on 14 May 2026. The long title to the Bill notes that it is “A Bill to make provision enabling the Secretary of State in certain circumstances to make regulations relating to the transfer of securities issued by, or property, rights and liabilities of, a steel undertaking; and for connected purposes.” The Bill is subject to the legislative consent process under Standing Order 29 . This is when the consent of the Welsh Parliament is sought by the UK Government to legislate on an issue in relation to Wales that has regard to devolved matters. The Legislative Consent Motion for the Bill was agreed in Plenary on 14 July 2026. Legislative Consent Memorandum – June 2026 On 23 June 2026, the Business Committee pre-emptively agreed (33KB) that the Legislation Committee and the Economy, Energy and Connectivity Committee should report to the Senedd by the morning of 14 July 2026. The Welsh Government laid a Legislative Consent Memorandum (PDF 248KB) on the Bill before the Senedd on 25 June 2026. The Legislation Committee laid its report (PDF183KB) on 14 July 2026. Business type: Legislative Consent First published: 25/06/2026 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47487', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 63.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', '', '', 'Legislation Committee; Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:51.027445', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47487', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7f84b8b83d621ef63f45', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Firstly, we acknowledge and appreciate that a supplementary budget like this is not the ideal time to make substantial changes. Also, we''re aware of the tightening fiscal environment and an inherited budget legacy. We have an ongoing shortfall in educational funding; that much is obvious. Whilst the £120 million allocation of additional funding for additional learning needs won''t solve all the challenges it faces, to reject it as part of the supplementary budget would be to play politics with school funding. Unlike others in the Chamber, we won''t be using this as a political football to gain some illusion of control.

 There is strong consensus among many in this Chamber about what we could do and what we can work to deliver for the people of Wales. It is time we genuinely worked together collaboratively and co-operatively to deliver these. Additional healthcare funding and the shift towards primary care is a step in the right direction, as is the £20 million capital funding for social housing. The housing crisis is a health crisis, and we know, as does the Government, that collective solutions will be more cost-effective, helping those who need it most, where they need it most, in their homes. This is something we will expect to see prominently feature in the next budget.

 Likewise, noting the £1 bus fare provision, we want to see investment in public transport go further, with cleaner, integrated, sustainable travel putting money back into people''s pockets as well as addressing the urgent need to transition away from fossil fuels.

 On climate, I''d like now to move to the most glaring omission in this supplementary budget. There is a small, but welcome, uplift in both resource and capital allowances to develop and implement climate change policy, green growth and environmental protection. But this funding is apparently ring-fenced for the Welsh Government energy service, not to address the climate change mitigation and adaptation. The Met Office yesterday released data showing that more than 2,700 people are thought to have died from heat-related causes during the May and June heatwaves in England and Wales. Of those, almost half died as a result of the extra heat caused by human-induced warming. The authors of this report said:

 ''It''s time we woke up to the fact that we now live in a country with dangerously hot summers. To protect people during future extremes, we must urgently adapt''.

 Our summers are 3-4 degrees Celsius hotter than they would have been without human-induced climate change. Last month, 35.9 degrees Celsius was recorded at Bute Park in Cardiff, breaking the previous June record of 33.7 degrees Celsius, set in 2000. That week, children were sent home from school. As a country, we are incredibly underprepared. The UK Climate Change Committee''s recent climate risk assessment found that critical action was needed in Wales to address health risks from heat. It estimates that UK-wide heat deaths could increase from around 1,400 to between 3,000 and 10,000 in the 2050s without further adaptation. The health toll is rising and it''s costing lives.

 This week, as was mentioned earlier today, North Wales Fire and Rescue Service have been tackling a major incident with the outbreak of wildfires near Conwy mountain. Families are having to flee their homes to safety. This is a frightening situation for all involved, and our thoughts are with anyone impacted by this devastating situation. Having declared a climate emergency all those years ago, where is the action to treat the situation as the genuine crisis that it is?

 For the people of Wales, these issues cannot wait until the next budget, or the next one. The climate change action fund has not changed but the reality of the situation has. Where will the money come from to address this unfolding crisis? The Government must provide assurances that climate mitigation and adaptation will be given the necessary prominence it needs, going forward, and within the next budget.

 Finally, I''d like to say, as a Member of a party that never whips its Members in any Parliament, we will be voting to support this budget. I believe that to do otherwise would be an irresponsible dereliction of duty. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Anthony Slaughter', '', '5025', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25725&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'social_housing; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Housing system', 'Sector bodies; Opposition housing leads', '', 'social housing; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.780409', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767972', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7fa4a933d9e627d0af0b', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'The programme that Natural Resources Wales have in north Gower is something that''s tackling exactly what it is that you''re talking about, and I just hope that''s something that you can support when they look for funding, please.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'John Davies', '', '4650', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1656&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.501315', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771832', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7fca6f406718b20d2a96', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Well, there we are. We can move on, then. [ Laughter .]', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6197&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.126140', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772138', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7fcadfef5172b07d6990', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Okay. Anna.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2372&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.637004', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771936', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('7fe9ab2270a7f93faa4e', 'committee_transcript', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. General scrutiny session with the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care, the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health and the Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health', 'Great. Thank you very much, and thank you to the committee for the opportunity to be here today. As Mabon mentioned, clearly, our work has focused in the first 100 days on realising the ambitions we set out. We are now, of course, steering the programme for government, which will be based on the manifesto. But, in terms of that preventative health element that I''m responsible for, you''ll be aware that it''s the first time we''ve had that kind of role in Government, so it''s incredibly important to set out the foundations in terms of how we''re going to take action across this term.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16251', '2026-09-17', 'Nerys Evans', 'Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health', '261', '', '', 'Health and Social Care Committee', '2. General scrutiny session with the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care, the Deputy Minister for Public and Preventative Health and the Deputy Minister for Social Care, Mental Health and Women’s Health', '16251', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16251?startPos=480&l=en', NULL, 17.2, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:13.860766', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16251&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769870', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('80302a6729725a7a88e5', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '1. What lessons has the Cabinet Minister learnt since failing to pass the first supplementary budget for 2026-27, and how will this inform the Welsh Government''s approach to future budgets? OQ64442', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Andrew R.T. Davies', '', '143', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', '', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:16.900089', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769513', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8070db987fed9e9db00e', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'One Bill that I will be very interested in is the tribunals Bill, which has its genesis in the last Parliament, although work is now going ahead at pace. The purpose of the Bill will be to create a new structure for all six silos, if you will, six various tribunals in Wales, together with implementing an appeal mechanism within Wales as part of that. It''s a big undertaking. It was initially introduced by the previous Government and is something we are keen to move forward with. It is almost directly opposite to what my colleague has said in terms of small, focused Bills, but there''s room for both. That''s the point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=472&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.116426', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770343', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('80a835d3540a9e97e118', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Maybe if I start with this one in terms of the ''possible'' question. It''s possible. I think we''ve all identified risks that have to be mitigated if that''s going to work effectively, and I think we would stress that they are live risks that are being managed through that co-ordination. But I think we''ve all flagged up as organisations that the pressure those small electoral registration teams are under is not insignificant, and, actually, the momentum and the resource that needs to be put in to make sure any issues they faced are removed and addressed, whether that''s from software providers being able to deliver on time, whether it''s in relation to the funding that''s available, I think it''s that responsiveness that''s really, really critical if we''re to see this be successful for 2027.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6112&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.542806', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769966', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('810db933bb1087cfb8c7', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Okay.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'John Davies', '', '4650', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3102&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.752637', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771907', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8120e18ed573f6ebdb64', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'And, as you say, I''m repeating myself, but the clear red flag and lesson of what''s happened in the south-west and now the north-west England, on BTV-3, is absolutely salutary for us all to learn from. 

 In terms of different factors that may be affecting vaccine uptake, I think, again, there''s no simple answer here. It may be a question of timing. There were extensive conversations going on between farming unions, veterinarians, clients and their veterinary practices, to look at the options around vaccine, the timings from the point of view of the production system, the business—the farm business. Because, as I say, it''s also got to be synchronised, if you will, or tailored from the point of view of what is best. And there are a range of factors, I''m sure, that are at play. 

 But, crucially, it''s a commercial decision that farmers will be taking, based upon their business, based upon a range of different factors, to use that vaccination, to get it into their stock, because it is the best insurance policy they can possibly have to protect against the devastating impacts that BTV-3 can wreak.

 And we know that there are over 600,000 vaccine doses for BTV-3 that have been prescribed in Wales to date. But I would absolutely say and would hope the committee would support us in this, Chair, from the point of view of ensuring that message is shared by everybody to protect stock by BTV-3 vaccination, for farmers and vets to take that commercial decision. I don''t know if it''s in your gift to do that, Chair.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Richard Irvine', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6333&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.158784', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772126', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('818ba8dab005681b082b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'I was delighted to hear yesterday of the Government''s commitment to introduce the community right to buy. There are good examples of market towns that succeed in creating energy and activity, through a number of indigenous businesses rooted there. In my own constituency, there are a number of examples in Machynlleth of community groups buying community assets, such as churches and pubs and so on, and keeping them going for the benefit of the community and the local economy. Can the Cabinet Minister make a statement on community right to buy? Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Elwyn Vaughan', '', '8371', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3643&l=en', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'Housing minister', '', 'community assets; community right to buy; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.029025', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768134', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('81bfbb423bb3c818263b', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'And to take that a step further, the flood forum work that we''re doing now in setting up a flood forum for Wales, that''s all about community resilience. So, they should be part of that catchment-based discussion as well, because those communities at the bottom of the valley are the ones that see the real difference when the extreme weather events hit.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3588&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.826302', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771817', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('81dc56c4479971d750e0', 'calendar', 'Senedd forward look', 'Finance Committee — 24 September 2026, 09.30', 'Finance Committee is scheduled to meet on 24 September 2026 at 09.30.
Meeting status: Confirmed; Hybrid
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=984&MId=16250', '2026-09-24', '', '', '', '', '', 'Finance Committee', '', '16250', '', '2026-09-24', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Finance Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.447990', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('82069dec4ef52b39c05d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Finally, on the first phase of the legislative programme, I turn to the economy. Whilst far too many major economic levers still reside in Westminster, we will not stop making the case for their transfer. But, as an immediate step, a tax maintenance Bill will continue Wales''s tax devolution journey. These proposals will help the Welsh Revenue Authority continue to administer the land transaction tax and landfill disposals tax effectively by making operational and technical changes to clarify, enhance and futureproof the Welsh tax Acts and to support information sharing between the WRA and local authorities.

 My Government will never place limitations on our level of ambition for Wales. The new national development agency will spearhead our mission to halve Wales''s productivity gap with the rest of the UK in the next decade. With the Minister for enterprise already having announced an expert advisory panel, there is considerable momentum behind this goal. We won''t take a final decision on the form of that agency until we''ve been able to consider the panel''s advice, but we will be ready to bring forward legislation should that be required.

 This practical approach will underpin our activity in a number of areas, including child poverty and the Welsh language. We will review the evidence, we will consider the options available, and we will bring forward legislation where that is what is needed to deliver the outcomes on the ground that we want to see.

 Before concluding, Llywydd, I''ll say a brief word about other legislation. It''s the combination of primary and secondary legislation that makes up the backbone of the developing Welsh statute book. The Government''s vision is not only to bring forward individual instruments that improve the lives of the people of Wales, but at the same time to create a body of Welsh law that is clear, coherent and accessible to those who rely on it. Central to this vision is our continuing programme of consolidation and codification of Welsh legislation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=8617&l=en', NULL, 198.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; fiscal_and_legislative_context; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Delivery bodies', 'Legislative commitment; Opportunity to influence', 'Land Transaction Tax; advisory panel; land transaction tax; legislative programme; will bring forward legislation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.967284', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767749', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('821206712827c70e9057', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'The proposal is to agree the motion. Does any Member object? [ Objection. ] I will defer voting under this item until voting time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26478&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.863209', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767979', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('823b91d331434da45a3f', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you very much. The third sector is an absolutely essential part of supporting all the work that we''ve discussed this morning. I will be talking to my colleagues in the Cabinet about this. In a few weeks'' time, I will be taking a paper to the Cabinet that deals with this specifically, because we have to consider the future relationship between the Government and the third sector, and also how we think about volunteering and so on. Of course, this is something that is underlined by our responsibilities under the Government of Wales Act 2006. So, I just want to state clearly for the record that I acknowledge how absolutely essential and important that relationship is, and that sector is, to the work of the Government.

 I do recognise, and I''m sure that all of us around this table could give examples of where things are tough for the sector. One of the challenges is the funding challenge, and that challenge of having to go through the process of seeking grants or funding, which can be an obstacle to the ability of that organisation to be successful and effective. So, I just want to assure you that I do acknowledge that and understand that. I do think that we have to get rid of those obstacles, as that would enable this sector to be prosperous and successful, and some important things are already happening in terms of this.

 In 2026-27, the current financial year, as part of a three-year agreement—which, as you say, is not always something that the sector sees—we are giving over £9 million to Third Sector Support Wales to help local groups, so that they are able to deal with fundraising and so on, and governance and so on, and the things that they need to do in order to be successful. And also, in order to enable people in this sector and these organisations to better cope with the requirements, perhaps in terms of governance or tendering, and so on, which they face, we have a programme called Newid, which is a £1.5 million programme that is a partnership between the WCVA, Cwmpas and ProMo Cymru, which seeks to offer support in terms of training, mentoring and—what''s the word I''m reaching for here—digital ability, to assist them in the digital sphere to try to improve the skills that they need for the world that we''re living in.

 But I fully accept your point, as I say, in terms of thinking about what we can do. The code of practice for funding that you referred to is an example of that desire that we have to be able to ensure that we spend money more effectively, more meaningfully and that it''s easier to access. So, I do commit—. We know that there is a need and we will engage with the sector to hear from them and to learn from them about the barriers and challenges they currently face, as you''ve described, in order to ensure that, as we design and roll out our policies, and any funding or money that''s backing those policies and projects, that we use the right financial methods for those organisations that we expect to deliver on those programmes.

 So, that point is absolutely vital and I do thank you for raising it. And if the committee more broadly has any views or evidence that they would like to share with us on that, as we develop this new relationship, which as I mentioned, which is cross-Government with the third sector, we would be grateful to receive that also.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=7140&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.324168', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771279', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8265ec078aacd636ffc7', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes, I hope so. Clearly, it needs to be the right kind of intervention and it needs to be affordable. But, certainly, I''m very aware of the damage caused by the fires. And I''m sure many of us, not all of us, maybe, but many of us saw the news reports when this was really flagged as an issue, because it''s taken thousands of years for those environments to be established and it took weeks for them to be destroyed. So, we have to, again, I think, redouble our efforts to avoid finding ourselves in this position in the first place. So, it''s a balance then, I think, in terms of investing in those particular programmes, but also investing in the need to protect them in the wider sense.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1831&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.530827', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772009', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('827ae64fd99e88d4af5a', 'research', 'Senedd Research', '“A generational challenge” – young people and the labour market', '“A generational challenge” – young people and the labour market

Published 13/07/2026 | Last Updated 13/07/2026

The number of young people who are not in education, employment or training (NEET) has been high on the political agenda over recent months. Each number represents a young person’s life and future.

Young people face a range of complex and interconnected barriers to accessing education and entering the workplace. Ahead of Wednesday’s Plenary debate on young people and employment, this article explores these challenges, the support available from governments to address them, and the changes needed to improve outcomes for young people.

There are three main sources of data published on young people who are NEET. The statistical first release (SFR), the annual population survey (APS), and pupil destinations from schools. According to the Welsh Government, the SFR is the “definitive statistical source” for estimates of NEETs; this is published annually. The APS is a rolling year annual estimate; which is published twice a year. Careers Wales carries out an annual survey of school leavers in the October; this data is used to calculate the proportion of young people who are NEET. It’s not advised to draw comparisons between the different data sources.

Annual Population Survey (APS) statistics estimate that the proportion of young people aged 16-24 in Wales who were NEET was 17% in the year ending December 2025; an increase of 5.5 percentage points over the previous year. Note, there is increased uncertainty around estimates from the APS.

The graph below shows the Statistical First Release (SFR) data on the proportion of young people who were NEET and whether they were unemployed (actively looking for work and available to start work) or were economically inactive (not looking for work and not available to start work).

Using the SFR measure, in 2024, the proportion of young people aged 16-24 who were NEET was 13.7%, which is 47,100 young people. Of those who were NEET, 14,300 were unemployed and 32,800 were economically inactive. Over recent years, young people in Wales who are NEET have been considerably more likely to be economically inactive than unemployed.

Source: StatsWales, Estimated 16 to 24 year olds not in education, employment or training in Wales by economic activity and age group

Being NEET, particularly for a long time, can have permanent ‘scarring effects’ on a young person. Recent research from academics at University College London found that, in midlife, someone persistently out of employment, education or training as a young person is:

The challenges young people face in Wales are similar to those across the UK. The interim report to the UK Government on young people and work by Alan Milburn (the Milburn review) highlights young people across the UK who are NEET are becoming detached from the labour market, and are not looking for work.

The big change identified by the Milburn review is the considerable increase over the past decade in the proportion of people who are NEET due to a work-limiting health condition. Over the same period, the percentage of young people in the UK who are NEET has gone from being around the European average to being higher than all EU Member States except Romania.

The Milburn Review also highlights that some groups of young people are particularly likely to be NEET – care leavers, carers, young mothers, and youth offenders. It finds that these groups are failed at transition points by the services which are supposed to support them into the labour market.

Both the UK and Welsh governments provide support for young people who are NEET. The UK Government provides support through job centres, and has also developed a range of initiatives that operate in Wales:

The previous Welsh Government launched the Young Person’s Guarantee (YPG) to provide everyone aged 16 to 24, living in Wales, with support to gain a place in education or training or help to get into work or self-employment. It aims to prevent young people from becoming NEET. The YPG covers a number of support packages including:', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/a-generational-challenge-young-people-and-the-labour-market/', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 15.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'data_and_evidence', 'Context', '', '', 'StatsWales', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:50.050173', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8288dc0956c3fe989e5f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Diolch, Lywydd. Thank you for the range of views on this supplementary budget from across the political parties, Members, and also for the questions and contributions from committee Chairs and committee members as well.

 To Reform, thank you for outlining your three red lines. We don''t agree with your first red line, but we are working on your other two red lines. Part of that is in this budget and part of that is in the work that the health Minister is doing. 

 To the Conservatives, I can, as I said, see the logic to part of your reasoning in opposing the previous Government''s budget, as restated in this budget. And just to Darren, I can count; a good state school education made sure of that. 

 I thank the Green Party for your support, unwhipped as it is, and your challenge to us in that contribution to do more on climate change. 

 To Labour—and I''ll start by saying that you''re lucky, Huw, that I''m old enough to pass over a slightly condescending comment on my influence in Government—however, I''m totally flabbergasted by Labour. Today, you have had the opportunity to support a £120 million ALN support package for our schools and councils. [ Interruption .] You could have—[ Interruption .] Did you put your name forward to speak in the debate? Go on, then, you''re the leader of your group.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', 'Cabinet Minister for Finance', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26024&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.808752', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768008', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('82ed77628efd327a6f4f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Tenure-neutral Retrofitting Programme', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'retrofitting', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.250550', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768195', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('82f117fd3c41641837f4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you, Minister, for your answer. So, I take that as a ''no'', but, don''t panic, I thought that would be the reason, or the answer. But my team and I have been hard at work collecting this data from councils. We take Cardiff Council as an example; I''m sure our friend in the corner there will be listening with interest to this. The figures I''ve obtained under the Freedom of Information Act 2000 are shocking. I have them right here, and they will be made public as we speak.

 So, let''s look at the council''s social housing stock of over 14,000 homes, where nationality is actually recorded. More than 31 per cent of lead tenants are foreign nationals. On the waiting list of over 9,000 properties, non-British applicants are 22 per cent of those recorded. And of the new tenancies handed out last year, almost 30 per cent of recorded allocations went to non-British applicants. Meanwhile, 35 per cent of those in temporary accommodation are foreign nationals, where the data is actually recorded. This equates to thousands of foreign national households in one city, which is being subsidised.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Francesca O''Brien', '', '12190', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4091&l=en', NULL, 148.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'private_rented_sector; homelessness; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Housing minister; Opposition housing leads', '', 'social housing; temporary accommodation; tenancies', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.074853', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768155', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('82f62922f4a802155e11', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Diolch yn fawr iawn. I hope you enjoy the rest of your day. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=7131&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.280307', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772092', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('83bc2425895a632fe522', 'research', 'Senedd Research', 'What does the Armed Forces Bill mean for Wales?', 'What does the Armed Forces Bill mean for Wales?

Published 13/07/2026 | Reading Time minutes

In the coming months, Senedd Members will be asked to vote on whether to grant consent for provisions in the UK Government’s Armed Forces Bill .

If passed, the Bill would introduce changes to the Armed Forces Covenant in Wales and establish a new Defence Housing Service with responsibility for improving service accommodation across the UK.

This article sets out why the Senedd is being asked to grant consent, and explores some key measures in the Bill which are relevant for policy-makers in Wales.

The Armed Forces Bill is a UK Government Bill. It was introduced in the House of Commons in January 2026, and is currently making its way through the scrutiny process in Westminster. The House of Commons Library has published a briefing explaining the key measures in the Bill.

The Bill primarily deals with defence issues which are reserved to Westminster , but it includes provisions which relate to devolved matters. Although the UK Parliament can still legislate on devolved matters, the Sewel convention provides that it does not normally do so without the consent of the Senedd.

The UK Government is seeking consent from the Senedd for provisions to expand the Armed Forces Covenant , which would affect the functions of Welsh Ministers and certain devolved public bodies. In its Legislative Consent Memorandum (LCM) for the Bill, the Welsh Government has argued that other aspects of the Bill should also require consent.

The Armed Forces Covenant is a statement of the moral obligation which exists between the nation, the government and the armed forces.

Currently, the law places a duty on certain public bodies to have due regard to the Covenant principles when carrying out particular functions (sometimes known as the Covenant Duty).

The Armed Forces Covenant Duty is a legal obligation which applies to specified bodies. When exercising certain functions, these bodies must have due regard to:

If passed, the Bill would extend the Covenant Duty to national and devolved governments.

Local authorities, education bodies and health bodies in Wales are already subject to this duty . However, the Bill effectively extends the duty by requiring all specified bodies to have regard to the Covenant when exercising any functions relating to an expanded list of 12 key policy areas .

According to the Explanatory Memorandum that accompanies the Bill, the aim of these changes is to ensure the needs of the armed forces community are considered by all relevant policymakers and decision makers.

There has been widespread support for an extension of the Covenant Duty during scrutiny of the Bill.', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/what-does-the-armed-forces-bill-mean-for-wales/', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd Research', '', '', '', NULL, 50.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', 'Legislative commitment', 'LCM; explanatory memorandum', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:49.551152', 'https://research.senedd.wales/research-articles/', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('83bdea6b5de00dfcf0c2', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Okay, Rebeca?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6472&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.606129', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769975', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('843317136a969b7f49bb', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'So, my team is responsible for—. We see all the LCMs across various different Bills. We''re not responsible for writing them, as such, but we have a kind of co-ordinating role in making sure that there is a consistent amount of information that goes into each LCM. We also have a very good relationship with the Table Office and their officials there to ensure that what we think is good enough is also good enough for admissibility purposes. So, we do all that background work before LCMs are formally laid. We seek to make sure that that is done across each LCM.

 The two-week period is, obviously, very difficult. Sometimes that bottlenecks at the end, which is always unfortunate, but we do try and make sure that that does happen for each and every instance. The longer a UK legislative programme goes ahead—. The last one was incredibly long—I think we laid something like 78 or 79 LCMs and SLCMs. So, there might be some inconsistencies in quality when there are that many, but it is something that we are conscious of and that we do work on.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Cara Thomas', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=3869&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'LCM; Legislation Committee; legislative programme', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.754168', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770436', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('84464038fd9baef1c8c4', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'That''s fine. You go wherever you like.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2076&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.588328', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771995', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('844bee4efdf821ceb561', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'That is something that we have heard, and we''ve heard it for several years. Our manifesto commits, and this Government commits, to ensuring that we protect the funding for those crucial specialist services. So, we are, as part of this work, going to be looking at the way that that funding is allocated. That is at the heart of this work, because these are the people who do this vital work. So, funding is a major part of the discussions on this.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5541&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.132109', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771246', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8469bd6e9af54b8d3991', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'No. I''m more than happy to receive any written correspondence. And just to reciprocate as well, thank you to the committee, and I look forward to engaging with you regularly over the coming months and years. I know there will be legislation, regulation and inquiries that you will be holding, and I very much look forward to having this dialogue continue in a positive and constructive manner.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=7106&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.273594', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772084', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('84752e4bf34482bcf3ae', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yes, I know.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3372&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.936089', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771195', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('84ca1a7b94a748a78878', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Lovely. Well, I think we''re about halfway through our session, Cabinet Minister, so I think it would be probably an appropriate time for us to take a break. So, if we have a break now, a comfort break for people, and we''ll be back in five to 10 minutes. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3676&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.840161', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771833', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('85a79a117878efe577b3', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee: Remit of the Committee', 'So, if we move to item 2: Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee—remit of the committee. The purpose of the item is to note the committee''s remit. I''m assuming that everybody has received papers in relation to that. And the remit is outlined paragraph 5 of paper 1. I''ll just quickly go through it. It''s to examine legislation and hold the Welsh Government to account by scrutinising its expenditure, administration and policy matters, encompassing, but not restricted to, local government, communities, housing, planning and regeneration. Does anybody have any comments with regard to that remit or the statement? Okay. All right.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16169', '2026-07-09', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee: Remit of the Committee', '16169', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16169?startPos=2539&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:13.632975', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16169&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767182', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('86068aacc748ec86d434', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Andrew.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=772&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.377988', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771782', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('861635ded673ea9945d9', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I''ve not seen the outcome of any work as yet in terms of any results from it, but I do know that there is work being undertaken in relation to looking at the effects on habitat through bodies like Natural Resources Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Rhodri Asby', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1992&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.562107', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772027', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('864f726029f04c85283d', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'So, it''s not my portfolio, but I think that principle is absolutely right. I''ve always said we have at the minute a hub-and-spoke model when it comes to energy, where you have large, centralised, big energy-generating hubs. That''s then distributed to the ends of the earth very ineffectively and inefficiently through transmission loss et cetera, which isn''t, I think, the best way necessarily to do it. We need to move to more of a spider-web situation, which is much more resilient, where you generate locally, you use locally, and if one of those hubs goes down, then there are other networks nearby that can kick in. The risk is that, if you are on a hub-and-spoke model, if one of those big generators glitches, then it''s a huge problem for half the country. There''s an ongoing discussion around that. I know local grids and things are being trialled. I would want more of that to happen here in Wales, but I''m definitely now straying into other portfolio areas, but there''s a strong case to be made for doing more of that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4991&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.965728', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771813', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('865e2874151685d3dc1d', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Well, I was chair of the cross-party group on human rights in the previous Senedd, and we held an inquiry and published a report on how, currently, people aren''t either aware of their rights or aren''t able to see those rights being deployed in their daily lives. So, we heard evidence from disabled people, we heard evidence from people and organisations looking at women''s rights; we looked at people from minority ethnic backgrounds. There were a number of stakeholders who participated, and they were able to provide specific examples of where things weren''t operating as they should. We heard very powerful evidence about the Stolen Lives campaign, for example. This was an example where there were people who had learning disabilities being kept, in contravention of their human rights, in hospitals that weren''t appropriate for them. Because there wasn''t provision for them to live independently, they were being placed, and locked, indeed, in institutions that didn''t respect their human rights.

 So, what I want this Bill to be is a kind of framework to help organisations in Wales to make the right decisions, to understand what those rights mean for people''s lives on a daily basis, to help to raise awareness amongst people about how they can receive remedy and redress when things go wrong. Because, we say that Wales has been in the vanguard when it comes to human rights in terms of how we pay attention to children''s rights, for example, but we often see decisions being made by public bodies and by Government that contravene those rights. So, I do think it''s about making it clear to people what they can expect, and thinking about that framework of the UN treaties is a way that we can ensure that there is awareness and that there is action. But I am very clear that I want to make it easier. I want to stop these problems from arising in the first instance. So, I hope that it will provide leadership and support to public organisations to ensure that they don''t contravene people''s human rights, with all of the implications that has for people in their daily lives.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3025&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Consultation open', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; inquiry', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.894990', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771240', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('865fb4848929c6d9d7ba', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yes, of course. The idea behind this is thinking about a new way of looking at crime prevention. We know that crime in a number of different ways—. Although we don''t have powers over criminal justice and policing, people in Wales do feel the impact, clearly, of offending and crime. So, what we want to do by working in partnership is look at how Wales can prevent that harm that is experienced by communities, by bringing together evidence and expertise from a number of public sectors.

 The focus of this new agency is to ensure that we tackle the causes of crime in the first instance—looking at early intervention, looking at working in stronger, more effective partnership, having that more co-ordinated approach across those services and agencies. Some of those have been devolved and some are agencies that are reserved, such as the police and so on. So, what we are trying to look at in terms of the outputs of this work is better co-ordination and cohesion through our communities and these public services in Wales, using data and evidence more effectively to identify the risks more swiftly and to ensure that we target interventions more effectively and earlier.

 We know that there is excellent practice in some areas. Some of you will have heard about the Cardiff model, for example, where they looked at where there was crime of a particular kind happening in specific areas, and then they asked, ''Well, why is that happening? Why are there thefts from this shop on this street and not from that shop on that street? What are the factors?'' Well, it could be, for example, things related to street lighting. It could be to do with CCTV being present, or where that street leads and whether it is somewhere the police patrol often. There are many different factors at play. By the same token, women''s safety. There are some areas where there are attacks that can happen specifically on women, and we need to look at the community infrastructure, and not just the geography and street lighting and so on, but also whether we provide safe access to bus and train stations, for example. So, it''s thinking about those questions to make those interventions and make decisions that would ultimately be able to prevent crime in the first instance. That''s a practical response. That''s the aim of the project.

 So, in terms of your question in terms of how this work is progressing, we are working with partners. We''ve already had a meeting with certain kinds of people—academics, representatives of the local authorities, representatives of the health service, representatives of the police forces in Wales—to have conversations about this. There is very wide-ranging support for this initiative. So, that''s where we are at the moment. I can''t give specific dates yet because what we are doing at the moment is deciding what the remit and structure of the agency will be. Before we decide that—and we are discussing that at the moment—that is the work that is being done and we haven''t yet reached a conclusion on that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6284&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.217929', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771282', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('87016c3da320df753560', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '[ Inaudible .]—as you said, with those particular demographics, it is having the local data available and that that local data is as up-to-date as possible to allow you to automatically register them. And I think the actual register, in an ironic sense, is probably the most up-to-date record that we''ve got in a local authority, in terms of the fact that you carry out a canvass every single year and the information is verified. And, as Sandra said, looking at alternative methods in terms of those, because having access to data that accurately reflects those high churn types of properties and things, is a challenge. There''s no two ways about that.

 I think the point around university students is quite an interesting point as well, as you said. It was interesting, during the pilot, when you had the data matching taking place in the May period, the risks that that sometimes can introduce as well. You would have automatically registered a higher volume of university students in one of the pilot areas, but, due to the nature, as you said, of student registrations, as soon as you''d put them on the register, they''d effectively moved by the time that you published the register. So, again, there are concerns around how different demographics interact, the availability of data that you''ve got that allows you to do that, and also the mechanisms that you have then to review the register. For types of properties like that, there''s a direct registration process that the Welsh Government are proposing, but there''s not a way of automatically removing those people that you can see where there are those high churn areas as well. And our concern is that you can end up then with register bloat, potentially, where you''re adding lots of people and so you''re getting a more complete register, but whether that register is more accurate is questionable, unless you''ve got the mechanisms to easily remove people and identify them to take them off the register. Again, the UK Government''s Representation of the People Bill is looking at introducing that side of it as well. So, we''ve got concerns, which I think is what is being stated here, about how you get these high churns on. It''s also how you get those high churns off to make sure that register accuracy is maintained across the board, and also then trying to find those data sources that more accurately help you identify—. For universities, it''s clear where that information might come from. But, for things like HMOs and things, it''s having access to information that local authorities have access to, to be able to accurately and quickly be able to do that as well.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5125&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'hmos_and_standards; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'HMOs; Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.370512', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769949', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8739833dc9aa7d4c671d', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thank you. Elyn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=580&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.767394', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769819', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('87bb74a6abe62a8b9c4e', 'press_release', 'Welsh Government — Announcement', 'People facing hardship in Wales to get emergency food and poverty support', 'People facing hardship in Wales to get emergency food and poverty support
Nearly £5 million invested in local authorities across Wales to tackle poverty Funding will support emergency food aid and long-term poverty prevention Extra money allocated to rural areas facing distinct challenges

Nearly £5 million invested in local authorities across Wales to tackle poverty Funding will support emergency food aid and long-term poverty prevention Extra money allocated to rural areas facing distinct challenges

People struggling with the cost of living will benefit from nearly £5 million in Welsh Government funding to local authorities this year.
The £4.8m investment will fund emergency food aid for people in crisis, alongside support to prevent poverty.
Local authorities can use the funding to strengthen community-based support networks like lunch clubs, social supermarkets and community cafes. They can also give direct grants to food banks facing increased demand.
The funding will also help tackle the root causes of poverty, enabling organisations to strengthen cost of living advice to families, helping people build lasting resilience, not just cope in a crisis.
Capital funding will help organisations buy fridges, freezers and cooking equipment, so they can store and distribute more food, including surplus supplies that would otherwise go to waste.
Additional money will also go to rural local authorities, recognising the added challenges of delivering support in rural areas.
Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality, Sioned Williams said:
"Nobody in Wales should have to go without food or struggle to make ends meet. This funding will help local authorities respond quickly to people in crisis right now, while also building the long-term support that helps families avoid hardship in future.
"We know the cost of living continues to put pressure on households across the country, and that''s why we''re targeting support where it''s needed most. We want every part of Wales to have the resources they need to support people through difficult times and build lasting resilience for the future."
Previous funding has helped projects like
Down to Zero
support people experiencing food poverty in Rhondda Cynon Taf (RCT).
Down to Zero
provide affordable, locally grown and pesticide-free produce straight to people''s doorsteps in RCT – delivering over 230 boxes of fresh, low-cost vegetables in RCT last year.
One of the site managers at Down to Zero, Llantrisant, Kevin Edwards said:
"As the Llysiau lead at Down to Zero, my role is to help with the general running of the site, and to run the Llysh bocs deliveries. At the moment I am delivering vegetable boxes to 40 families through RCT.
“The scheme is to help families who are struggling with food poverty with deliveries every three days. It’s heartbreaking to hear that families are struggling to feed their children, so I’m really proud to be involved in a scheme that is trying to reduce food poverty.”
The funding forms part of a wider package of the Welsh Government''s cost of living support, which helps households manage financial pressures, maximise their incomes and reduce everyday costs.
Support includes; the Single Advice Fund, the Discretionary Assistance Fund, the Warm Homes Programme, fuel bank vouchers, free school meals and the Bwyd a Hwyl Food and Fun school holiday food and enrichment provision.', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/people-facing-hardship-in-wales-to-get-emergency-food-and-poverty-support', '2026-09-17', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Deputy First Minister; Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality - Sioned Williams', '', '', NULL, 81.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'energy_efficiency; welfare_and_support', 'Housing system; Property & energy', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Warm Homes; discretionary assistance fund', 0, '2026-09-18T11:37:44.620890', 'newsroom:people-facing-hardship-in-wales-to-get-emergency-food-and-poverty-support', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('87c6b90a31871af5cde0', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'Thank you. Well, you''ve identified there that we need to do this work in a phased manner, and that''s what we''ve been clear about from the outset. You''re right to underline that the commitment that the previous Government made to providing childcare places through the Flying Start scheme for those aged two years for 12.5 hours hasn''t been delivered. That''s what the funding in the supplementary budget is going to enable us to do, namely to complete that step. That''s the first step as we build towards our ambition of providing 20 hours of childcare for every child in Wales from nine months to four years of age. This is the important first step. As this work has already been started, the funding in the budget is going to help us to reach that target of delivering that in full, and then, of course, to extend and build on top of that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=86&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.446666', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766796', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('87cb116f6202fa633b2f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Thank you. James Evans, as Chair of the Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee. James Evans.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25541&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.758967', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767968', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('87e14aa429a7d082620e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', 'Diolch, Lywydd. I''d like to declare an interest, as I have a beautiful 11-month daughter who will be going into childcare next month. But I''d like to thank the Government and the Deputy First Minister for her statement today and all the work that''s clearly being done on this policy. I don''t think we can overstate how transformational this policy is going to be, and I think, when people ask what has Wales done differently and what has devolution achieved, previously, they might have pointed to free prescriptions or the 5p bag charge, and I think, at full roll-out, I really believe it''s going to be this policy. But it needs to be done right—no pressure there.

 One issue that''s being raised with me a lot is general workforce capacity for the expansion, and specifically Welsh-medium provision. I know from my own experience that one nursery I approached in Cardiff was at full capacity until 2027, and there are not many Welsh language providers already. 

 And then I''ve also had providers raise issues specifically with support for younger babies, from nine months until two years, and I was assured in your statement today that that''s going to be in the later part of the roll-out, which will allow for this capacity building up. But I just wonder if you could provide some clarity there. I think investment in the supplementary budget is obviously going to really help this as well. But yes, once this—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Kiera Marshall', '', '12166', '', '', 'Plenary', '5. Statement by the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality: Update on Early Years Childcare and Play', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=13514&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.512393', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767810', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('881cfce40c11e19e2659', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I think you make a very valid point and a very fair point. It''s a fair challenge. It''s one that we need to consider and reflect upon. But that''s the change in mindset that we need to see happening on a societal basis. And, yes, challenge accepted. Good point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3660&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.837259', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771828', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('889b5e4a44610ca8c727', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Jayne Bryant.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1470&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.718427', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771227', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('88e8d62dcb4ed35da749', 'consultation', 'Senedd — Committee work', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 'Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee

Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Forward work programme – Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Issue Details Issue History Related Meetings The forward work programme of the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee sets out the work which the Committee is intending to carry out in the following weeks and months. Any documents published indicate the current intentions of the Committee at the time of publishing, however the programme may change at any time to respond to external events. On the 9th July 2026 the Committee held its first meeting . During the meeting Members discussed options for determining a strategic approach to the work within their remit, including how to set out priority areas for scrutiny over the term. To help inform these discussions and to shape the Committee’s forward work programme, Members agreed to launch a consultation on priorities for the Seventh Senedd. Consultation: Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee The Committee is seeking views from people and organisations on the issues they believe it should prioritise in the Seventh Senedd. Further information on how to submit your views is available on the consultation page . Please share this consultation with anyone who you think would be interested in submitting their views. Business type: Other First published: 11/06/2026 Consultations Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee (running until 14/09/2026) Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', '2026-08-04', '', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '', '', '', NULL, 98.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; consultation; consultations; seeking views', 0, '2026-08-04T14:02:52.205212', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=47562', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('88ef50f77ebdafdfab1c', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Well, you have a strategy and you have action on the ground, and I think it''s about striking that balance between having that ambition and having that bigger picture approach, but then having bespoke actions sat underneath it. We have a plethora of legislation in the first place. We have the Well-being of Future Generations (Wales) Act 2015, which sort of touches on this, we have the Environment (Wales) Act 2016, which was passed here, and we have the UK Climate Change Act 2008 as well. There are a lot of expectations in terms of reporting and monitoring and acting as well. When I was a member of this committee, one of the comments regularly was that there was so much happening it was really difficult to see a route to achieving not only the biodiversity goals, but also the net-zero goal as well. So, hopefully, our climate and nature action plan will distil much of that into a coherent document that people can hold us accountable to, but also see in terms of action happening on the ground. Rhodri, is there anything I need to add on that?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3324&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.791326', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771883', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('88f59b01aafff392434f', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'You''re entirely right. The charter, as it currently stands, is something that all local authorities have agreed that they support. That is, that charter is to do with trying to ensure that Welsh benefits, as we call them—the things that the Welsh Government does in order to try to support families in Wales—reach people in a simpler way. It can be very different across local authorities. We know that many people who are eligible for these payments don''t claim them—perhaps because of a lack of awareness, perhaps because it''s difficult and complex. So, that work has been going forth.

 I''ve been clear that we need for that also to be a focus for this Government. We need to—. In our first 100 days, we commissioned independent academics from Cardiff University to look at our Welsh system in its entirety—to review that. That evidence will now look across 15 programmes that offer types of support, in order to assess how effective they are, and also how they''re delivered, what the eligibility is—all those complexities, I believe, that will be crucial if we want to see more streamlining in regard to that system.

 So, we expect the report on that to come out by the end of the year, and that will assist us, then. We have a network that is looking at this, which includes people who are experts in this field, but also local authorities, to ensure that we can get to it on this work. I do think that it''s absolutely essential that we make sure, in these times, that the support that is available reaches the people who need it. I don''t know, Claire, if you want to speak a little about the work of the group on this.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2386&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.827183', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771197', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('89502dd478a386afb2a0', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', 'Now, younger generations today may want all of the same things that we wanted back in the days. I mean, I remember my first official paid job when I was 17 or 18 in the Sweater Shop in town. I was paid £2.65 and it really was an awful job. But the first thing that I got to do was to buy my first pair of Nike trainers, because up until then, it was Matchstick all the way, because that was what my parents could afford to buy me from Woolworths. But, of course, young people now want the same things. They want to make their own money. They want to make sure that they can pay their way in life and, of course, start building a future, not only for themselves, but also for many others. Many of them have caring responsibilities. They want to be able to buy a house or even start a family.

 Now, Llywydd, as the former health Secretary Alan Milburn has set out in his review, we risk a lost generation of young people. One in eight between the ages of 16 and 24 are not in employment, education or training, otherwise known as NEETs, and I was shocked, really, to find that the data from the end of last year showed that the situation in Wales is actually even worse. One in six young people are not in work, not in education or training, and that should really wind each and every one of us up and make us angry enough to want to do something about it and to work together on these issues, because youth unemployment is not a fringe issue. It is actually the symptom of a systemic failure, bigger than any one policy, bigger than any department or Government, and clearly, now, it demands an even bigger and better response.

 Now, I saw during my time at TUC Cymru that the last Welsh Government took some really positive steps. The young person''s guarantee that was rolled out, when we were coming out of the COVID pandemic, helped around 10,000 young people to get into work; 30,000 apprenticeships were started; and more people were helped to further their education or even start their own business. But clearly, though, that guarantee hasn''t been universally effective. So, it hasn''t done all the things that we were hoping to do. Only around half of the people that were supported through these various schemes actually went on to achieve a positive outcome. And that''s just the people who were even aware that this support existed in the first place and were able to take advantage of it and to navigate through it as well. But what about the people who weren''t aware, who weren''t able to navigate the systems that were set up?

 Now, as set out by the previous skills and social partnership Minister, Jack Sargent, the various schemes that make up the young person''s guarantee are going to be replaced by a new employability support programme, starting next year. I think that if we do this right, it genuinely can be a golden opportunity for us to do things differently here in Wales, to take what works and to streamline it with a real laser focus on moving young people into good, fair work jobs and other opportunities. The UK Government also operates a number of schemes for young people in Wales. The youth jobs grant, which started last month—that gives a real financial incentive for some employers to hire young people who have been out of work and are claiming universal credit. And, of course, there is the economic inactivity trailblazer scheme, which has been piloted in Blaenau Gwent, Denbighshire and Neath Port Talbot, and is currently being reviewed, as I understand it. This scheme, which looks at the underlying big causes of long-term unemployment, gives people the tailored support that they actually need, whether that''s for mentoring, counselling or any other kind of special support that that person may need.

 Now, despite all of the good work, all of the stuff that we''ve done so far, the issue with youth unemployment is not unique to Wales, but we can lead from the front when it comes to finding those solutions and implementing them. And we have to start, of course, with the evidence. Let''s listen to the people who know best, and that is the young people who are actually at that sharp end, who are facing these real challenges on a day-to-day basis, because we don''t have all of the answers, and that is the truth. Some people, young people in particular, are being let down more than others.

 The latest stats, the official stats from the Welsh Government, show that 40 per cent of people with disabilities between the ages of 19 and 24 are not in education, employment or training. We know it''s not because they lack skills or aspiration or any of those things. It is actually a failure, an abject failure of society, a lack of opportunity and a lack of proper support that''s being given. In reality, one in three disabled workers are not getting the reasonable adjustments that they actually need in the first place. Relatively few public sector organisations even have the highest level of Disability Confident accreditation. And according to Mencap, 86 per cent of young people with learning disabilities want to be in paid work, and in reality, it''s only 5 per cent—5 per cent—who actually are. This is mind-boggling, and that''s just not right.

 So, yes, while we have these schemes and they are transforming some lives, there are clearly gaps in the provision, and we know they exist and we need to do something about them. Now, when young people with disabilities consistently say that the systems that they''re meant to be using, that are meant to be there—. Well, they say, ''They''re not actually built with us in mind.'' And that''s not an opinion, that''s their own individual lived experience. These are the people who know the issues best, and the systems would work better if we did a better job of listening to the people who use them. So, that should always be our starting point—for this Welsh Government, for all of us in this Senedd, we really need to, always, get out into our communities, listen and learn and act.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Young people and employment', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=8103&l=en', NULL, 22.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', '', '', 'universal credit', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.445142', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768228', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8991ec36be9e35371556', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I mean, not specifically on that point necessarily, but that is a discussion we''re having, and I''m actually meeting with the planning Minister next week, I think, to discuss some of these issues. The other one as well, of course, is looking at planning in a rural context, and the potential to unlock some of the opportunities that might be there if we get the system right. So, there''s an ongoing discussion to be had there. You''re never going to iron out those contradictions totally because planning is about striking the balance with different influences and different factors that you have to consider. But where there is more that we can do, I''m very much happy to have that discussion.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4697&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.912927', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771950', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('89a2139a7955ec9ecdb7', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'But I would just like to be absolutely clear. I know it might sound like semantics, Chair, but we have our technical advisory group, who are our independent scientific experts. It does what it says on the tin; it''s a technical advisory group that looks at the evidence. It is the programme board who will be bringing forward their paper, supported by the evidence from the technical advisory group. And, as the Cabinet Minister rightly says, the programme board''s paper, and the Government''s response, are anticipated by the end of the calendar year.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Richard Irvine', '', '', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6199&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.127792', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772139', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('89b70f54bde56cfc1010', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'There are two other Members who want to speak, but just on this point directly, Cabinet Minister, it''s been raised already in the Chamber about whether Welsh Government is looking at bringing forward payments to support farmers early, because people have to wait for the funding and the support they receive to come through, but if that money was received earlier, it could help people to get through the winter. Is that something that''s under active consideration from the Government at this current time?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2237&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.609058', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772048', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8a3c5a0ea90532be0d60', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'I don''t know the answer to that question, but knowing that the UK Government have got direct registration and direct alteration principles and are looking to pilot in the very near future as well, the system will look different to what is being proposed by the Welsh Government currently. And as Ian said, there will then be this sort of friction, potentially, that that system introduces national data sets into the equation, which then changes fundamentally what that means. If you''re looking then to pilot the UK system within Wales, then you''ve got this dual process that butts heads a little bit in terms of that. I take Ian''s point completely that each Government has its own legislative capacity to decide what is best, but given that UK Government are also looking at this, there are definitely benefits to looking at how the two systems integrate better together before proceeding with implementation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=7283&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.749803', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769980', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8abc3102d5f4d6c5cc65', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'But the point is that the Counsel General is here to serve the Senedd, right? And, with respect to my friend, whatever the political view is, I think it makes good sense to liaise with all Members of the Senedd in order to take their views on board in early course, so that then we can look forward. And at the end of the day, experience shows me that if there are more people looking at it, it very often ends up being better legislation. I''m not just saying this in order to be popular before this committee; it''s something I''ve been discussing with my officials already and something I will adhere to without fear or favour, or any political interference.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1871&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.368463', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770394', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8ad1ef7878724ac1ff25', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I''m Elfyn Llwyd, Counsel General. To my right, I have Dylan Hughes, who is the First Legislative Counsel responsible for drafting on behalf of the Welsh Government. And to my left, we have Cara Thomas, who is a member of staff, who does a great deal of excellent work, and Sophie Brighouse, whom you''ll know very well, I''m sure, as she''s been here for a long time and is crucially important to everything that we do. And Dafydd Trystan.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', 'Counsel General', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=171&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.054317', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770347', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8ae239334c8fb5647936', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'Thanks for your response. Cabinet Minister, households across Wales are facing further cost-of-living pressures as prices keep rising. Also in Plaid''s ''The First 100 Days'' document was a commitment to review Welsh benefits such as the discretionary assistance fund. The Welsh Conservatives have always advocated for struggling families to be able to access the support that they''re entitled to, something that sadly did not happen under Labour. In 2023, the Welsh Government made £90 million available to support people with the cost of living when it came to fuel, yet less than £65 million had been spent. Whilst 427,000 households were eligible for support, only 316,000 households applied. Labour failed to properly inform the people of Wales about the assistance that they could receive, and I sincerely hope that doesn''t happen here under Plaid Cymru''s watch. So, what plans does the Welsh Government have to review the Welsh benefits system and help prevent households from being in crisis in the first place?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Natasha Asghar', '', '336', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=1729&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'discretionary assistance fund', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.622013', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766855', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8afa7ed23b2fcff181ee', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Lovely, thank you. And I think I''ll just put on record, on behalf of the whole committee, our thanks to all those emergency services, farmers and community groups across the country who all came together to tackle these wildfires. If it wasn''t for some of the general public at large, I dread to think where we''d have actually been, because I don''t think the emergency services could have managed it all on their own. So, thank you to all those people who helped.

 We''ll now move on to some other areas of questions that Members have got an interest in to ask you questions on. The first area will be on biodiversity. And I know Niamh has got some questions she''d like to ask you on biodiversity. So, I''ll hand over to you, Niamh. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2789&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.700984', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771985', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8b1ae49fea50776cabd5', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I''ll bring Vikki in now. I think it''s on this point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=5832&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.076708', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772067', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8ba1b54e8931b67d2dd2', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'A policy question for you on this, Minister; it''s probably not for the chief vet. Many people vaccinate against different things, obviously on animal welfare grounds, for example, people vaccinate for pasteurella and all different types of things because of animal welfare issues. If this is going to become, as we''re seeing with the warming climate, a regular event all the time, when does this stop being an animal management issue and then fit into an animal welfare issue, if we''re losing huge numbers of stock across the country that people aren''t going to be compensated for if they die? Maybe insurance companies won''t pay out if people are not vaccinating anyway. So, when does the policy element step in to say, ''Well, if people aren''t vaccinating, this then becomes an animal welfare matter''?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6503&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.184109', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772130', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8bcc24fd748a705fc8bf', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'That''s okay. Before we go on to the voluntary sector, Jane Dodds.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6809&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.283938', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771259', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8c21534fec8497f1fd8c', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much. ''Planning Policy Wales'' is kept under continual review to ensure that it remains effective, relevant and responsive to emerging evidence. Officials are currently reviewing planning guidance, and they are doing so to identify which aspects may need to be updated in future to ensure that the planning framework reflects the priorities of this Welsh Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4834&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning guidance; planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.159197', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768142', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8c480772dd7a28c40dc7', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes, just on that, looking at the graphs that we''ve been provided with: fuel supply and electricity supply are half of those reserved matters. But, of course, it strikes me that we could perhaps reduce those considerably by increasing the amount of renewable energy we generate here in Wales, decarbonising domestic heating and decarbonising private transport as well. Those are things that we still have the levers, don''t we, to change, even though they''re reserved.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Paul Rock', '', '12176', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4502&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.884432', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771922', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8c4b294fa7ef34391808', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'So, if I could explore briefly a couple of points around the notification period to opt out, because you''ll know that, originally, the pilots were moving forward with a 60-day opt-out. There have been various recommendations to go to a 28-day opt-out, and then there is a set of concerns from similar people saying, ''Well, yes it''s a good idea, but what about the safeguarding side of that?'' And I just wondered if you could give us a reflection on the benefits and potential drawbacks of that shorter 28-day period as you see it. That would be really handy.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4457&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.263619', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769907', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8c8baa9da0538b67cd7e', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I think, as Sophie''s already mentioned, what you have here is a programme that we''ve been working on with colleagues. There are issues around legislative capacity, there are issues around scrutiny, and, therefore, we''re making sure that we timeline that in a sensible and coherent way, so we don''t suddenly introduce a raft of legislation by the same Minister or in the same week. That could be rather challenging, I think, shall we say? So, that''s the thought process going on there. So, we are working on it. Colleagues are working on very carefully timelining that with a view to the role of the Senedd, the role of Ministers and the role of those who will be supporting the drafting and the development of the legislation.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=540&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.131285', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770366', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8cb4fa9486ea37e2ed35', 'consultation', 'Welsh Government — Consultation', 'Stationary vehicle engine idling: local authority guidance', 'Stationary vehicle engine idling: local authority guidance
We want your views on draft guidance for local authorities (LAs) on tackling unnecessary vehicle engine idling.
How to respond
Consultation ends:
4 December 2026
Consultation launched:
11 September 2026
Consultation description
The guidance is designed to promote a consistent and effective approach across Wales. It provides:
advice and examples to support LAs in developing and delivering local communications and initiatives to discourage stationary engine idling.
guidance on the use of enforcement powers in the context of new regulations, which come into force on 30 September 2026.
