11 September 2026', 'https://www.gov.wales/stationary-vehicle-engine-idling-local-authority-guidance', NULL, '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', '11 September 2026 Open consultation Environment and climate change', '', '', '2026-12-04', 84.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'local_government_enforcement', 'Housing system', '', 'Consultation open', 'consultation; enforcement powers; we want your views', 0, '2026-09-11T15:28:30.254149', 'consultations:stationary-vehicle-engine-idling-local-authority-guidance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8cbbd1910efc7640500e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'The new legislation that I was involved in drawing up in the last Senedd is more far-reaching than the legislation in England, which came into force some years ago. We are consulting on new regulations. I pushed the previous administration to include the ability to do that. So, we are consulting on new regulations that will mean that those people living in buildings that haven''t been remediated, or who are facing additional costs because their service charges have increased because of work that has to be done—those people will have a route to tribunal, once those new regulations are in place. Now, that is a significant step forward, and it''s going to take a little time, a few months, before we bring those regulations into force. I hope that everyone will support—I''m sure that you will support—those regulations.

 There are three aspects of this in reality, aren''t there? There is the new legislation in Wales, but there is also the remediation work, which is extremely slow in happening; it is extremely slow—we accept that. I have invited the companies that are continuing with this work to meet with me next month, where they will get a very clear message that this Government insists that that remediation work should be accelerated, and that the pace should go up.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3846&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold', 'Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'service charges', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.051321', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768156', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8d039e66f62f452f9ca8', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'With apologies to my friends and colleagues. I was reminded by Professor Thomas Watkin, who is another man who made a significant contribution to the development of Welsh law, who has a great interest in these things, and made a significant contribution in Bangor University in the development of the school of law. So, my apologies to my friends and colleagues in Bangor University.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4656&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.912248', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770603', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8d234cba82b041341ae0', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'That''s okay—you carry on.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=5249&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:10.053566', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770549', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8d7863d89a41e468f7db', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Tom might be able to say a little bit more about this as well. But I think, at the moment, the data matching powers and processes for Welsh registers is limited to data that''s available locally to Welsh electoral registration officers. So, they won''t necessarily have availability of data at this stage of things like DWP data.

 I think in response to your second question about whether more is better in this case, partly that will depend on the quality of the data as well. So, how well formatted is the data? Does it have things like unique property reference numbers? How are the names and addresses formatted, so that it''s simple, or as simple as possible, to match them to the electoral register? It may be, for example, the case that a registration officer could identify a data source that looks helpful in theory, but actually the way the data is formatted means that it would be disproportionately complicated to make that match with the electoral register to be able to identify entries that could be followed up. I don''t know, Tom, if you wanted to add anything on either of those points about data sources.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2536&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.079800', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769894', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8daa79479597fd5fd5b5', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'We''re talking about 22 local authorities, aren''t we, who are all, as you are fully aware, uniquely different, and in essence the ability to manage those risks both in a planned way but also to respond to those risks that the smaller teams bring out. The support that''s provided is really critical. It will only be achieved if a whole-council approach is taken in those 22 authorities and particularly in those smaller authorities, which is why that emphasis is needed at the top level of the council.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6680&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.643299', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770257', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8ed256c6fbbf8bb522c6', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Good morning and thank you for being here.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Kiera Marshall', '', '12166', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=274&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.075389', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770358', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8f2409df594e2329c366', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thanks. That''s okay for me, Chair. I don''t know if anybody else wants to come in on that one.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=478&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.747689', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769828', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8f5c66f5b2196d2a1ed5', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes, thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6637&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.203504', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772107', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8f7f000783a544d9e16a', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Well, Llywydd, former US President Lyndon B. Johnson once said that the first rule of politics is to be able to count. It''s a simple rule, but it''s one that the Cabinet Minister for Finance clearly didn''t think about when she was putting her supplementary budget together. We were, of course, expecting Labour to capitulate, but the feud between the two left-wing parties in the Senedd appears to be continuing. But the political reality is this: the arithmetic—[ Interruption .] I''ll happily take an intervention. Give me one second. The political reality is this: the arithmetic in this Senedd means that Welsh Government budgets and Welsh Government legislation cannot be agreed without cross-party agreement. You have to secure a majority of the votes cast. That agreement has not been secured, and it''s bonkers to bring something to the Senedd for a vote without securing it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Darren Millar', '', '171', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=24288&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.620828', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767906', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8fcefa61aa84b52480b4', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes, thank you. And where the peatland has been damaged by the wildfires over the summer, like in Llanwonno in my constituency, my understanding from speaking to local land managers there is that that damaged and compacted peat can now form an additional fire risk unless mitigation measures are carried out. And obviously, Welsh Government through many years has invested a lot in peatland restoration. Some of that now has gone up in smoke—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1790&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.522575', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772008', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('8fe2c0bdfc55a030ce77', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question (tabled)', 'OQ64442', 'What lessons has the Cabinet Minister learnt since failing to pass the first supplementary budget for 2026-27, and how will this inform the Welsh Government''s approach to future budgets?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OrderPaper/OralQuestions/16-09-2026/', '2026-09-09', 'Andrew R.T. Davies', '', '', '', 'Pen-y-bont Bro Morgannwg', 'Plenary', 'To the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '', '', '2026-09-16', 35.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-09-11T15:12:01.842409', 'orderpaper:2026-09-16:OQ64442', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('901a87a30c0ef12a2517', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch. Bore da, Minister. Last week, the Welsh Government announced some extra funding for emergency food aid for people in crisis and support to prevent poverty across Wales, which is obviously very welcome. You''ll know that, from the index of multiple deprivation, relative deprivation in Wales is heavily concentrated in urban communities, major cities, the south Wales Valleys and specific coastal areas. In that announcement you also added extra to rural areas. Can you just explain why that was done, based on the fact that there''s more poverty in urban areas? Out of income-deprived individuals in Wales, 77 per cent live in urban areas compared to 13 per cent in larger rural areas and 10 per cent in smaller rural areas. I know there is rural poverty as well, but I''m just looking at this particular point. Was that money that you''ve announced extra, or was it top-sliced off the way that you usually distribute funding across to local authorities in Wales? Is this something we can expect from future announcements as well?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jayne Bryant', '', '5038', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1471&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Sector bodies; First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.719985', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771230', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('90b332233edb5611e82b', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'You say, Counsel General, that the use of secondary legislation is becoming more prominent, I suppose, as time goes by. Is that as a direct result, do you think, of, how can I put it, lazy drafting of legislation? It concerns me that a lot of power is given to Welsh Ministers, power that the Senedd is not responsible for after the event, as such. So, I just wondered what your view was on that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Adrian Mason', '', '12181', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1362&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.278934', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770352', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('90e68b4da57502173bfb', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Thank you, Minister. I''m glad to hear it''s so full. Your published ministerial responsibilities include official statistics, including the census, social research and the national survey for Wales. Effective government depends on understanding how Wales is changing and ensuring that future planning reflects that evidence. What research has your department undertaken into the demographic changes in Wales since the implementation of the nation of sanctuary policy and how are those findings being incorporated into the evidence used to forecast future demand for housing, NHS services, school places, transport infrastructure and other public services across Wales? If those demographic changes are not being factored into Government planning, how can you be confident that your forecasts accurately reflect the future needs of Wales? This is not being divisive or any other adjectives you may wish to throw at our party; the people of Wales deserve to understand how the Government determines the future requirements of our nation, and your portfolio is central to ensuring that those decisions are informed by robust evidence and accurate data. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Steven Rodaway', '', '12191', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=3705&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'data_and_evidence', 'Context', '', '', 'National Survey for Wales', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.518537', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767584', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('91ac048388126aa3214f', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I haven''t, no, but you''ve piqued my interest there, and I''d be really keen to learn more about what they do, because, as you say, there are a number of challenges that we face, and we really do need to be able to develop that expertise, lean heavily on that expertise, and make sure that we can implement some of the innovation that they''re leading on more widely.

 I visited last week Aberystwyth University, where there''s a lot of work happening in terms of animal health. The veterinary work that''s happening there, the development of laboratory capacity—there are all sorts of opportunities around these academic hubs, and the more that''s happening in that space, the more learning we can take, and the better off, hopefully, we''ll all be, as long as we can implement that in a practical way.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=3143&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.759545', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771913', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('922c89e0bb811400c01f', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'The first one is about areas with high turnover. These are areas with students, but also areas with lots of houses in multiple occupation. And Cathays in Cardiff, Treforest in Pontypridd, Uplands in Swansea meet those criteria. How are you going to make sure you get those matched as people move?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Mike Hedges', '', '332', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4965&l=en', NULL, 138.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'hmos_and_standards; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; houses in multiple occupation; multiple occupation', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.348347', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769947', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('92317fbe6456b5973a39', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Away you go, you''re fine.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Irranca-Davies', 'Llywydd', '5053', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26315&l=en', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.848197', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768004', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('926ada5981cd01ed57cc', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', 'As we know, high streets are at the heart of our communities, and the Cabinet Minister''s announcement today to cut business rates by 30 per cent for hospitality and leisure businesses across Wales is a very welcome step to rebalance the non-domestic rate system. In addition to today''s announcement, can you confirm whether any consideration has also been given to the small business rates relief threshold and, as Sam has already mentioned and you''ve touched upon, business rates holidays for new businesses, both of which would be a boost for our local entrepreneurs?

 Whilst there would be a cost to such measures, one way to cover this, perhaps, would be to consider different valuation methods or multipliers for certain types of property, for example, data centres, which do not currently have their own classification. This way, some of the extractive profit generated from these properties could be retained and instead used to benefit local communities.

 I look forward to working with the Cabinet Minister on longer term reforms that fall outside today''s scope of this announcement.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Nick Carter', '', '12173', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=11008&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.224591', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769233', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('927341cbe537d11b0e98', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I''ll tell you at 11:00. [ Laughter .]', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2307&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.625258', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772056', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('928c85a725b67c502a33', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Thank you very much for the question. The town centre taskforce that we''re going to be announcing more about quite soon will address the structural challenges facing town centres, including looking at business rates and planning reform, and by working collaboratively with partners and maintaining a strong focus on delivery to help town centres adapt, attract investment and activity and thrive in the years ahead.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Sian Gwenllian', '', '4981', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=3476&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; planning_system', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'Housing minister', '', 'business rates; planning reform', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.016994', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768147', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('929cdc7ccc2b08d792e6', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Just maybe a couple of comments. Obviously, you''ve got some brilliant lived experience from Sandra in Powys, who''s obviously undertaking that role on a daily basis, and Clare representing electoral administrators. I guess, as an electoral management board, we both have a statutory role in terms of elections, but also a co-ordinating role. So, I suppose I''m going to give more of an overview, and I think one of the things Sandra''s pointed out is that that was successful in Powys. Not all electoral teams are the same in terms of their size and capacity. I think it''s fair to say that they''re an extremely skilled workforce, so they have the skills to do it. I think, as Clare''s described, it''s the speed and the timing that''s the issue that needs careful management.

 As an electoral management board, we''ve co-ordinated an impact assessment to try and make sure those impacts are really, really clear, and there is a Welsh Government AVR working group in terms of making sure those impacts are mitigated. I think that''s just really critical that there''s momentum on that—so, those risks around software, timescale and also funding. And we''re grateful that funding has been given to support the additional resources that are required, but it remains a live risk in terms of delivery for the 2027 period, and I think just being tight around managing that live risk is really critical and important.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4358&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; working group', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.243156', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769943', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('92da05bbff080e35475b', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Adrian wants to come in here, just on that point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4644&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.905548', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770601', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('92e5170567ac8fcf2eb7', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Statement by the First Minister: Weather, wildfires, drought and resilience', 'I would like to declare an interest, having worked for South Wales Fire and Rescue Service for over 20 years. First Minister, I thank our firefighters, control staff and support teams. On 13 August, joint fire control handled over 1,000 calls. At one point, south Wales crews faced 14 wildfires simultaneously. Afan Ogwr Rhondda needs well-trained crews, the right equipment and reliable water supplies, even in drought. On 23 July, your Cabinet Minister announced a review of firefighting capability and costs. Will you publish a costed plan by 31 October, agreed with all three fire services, identifying what must improve, who is responsible, and what will be delivered before next spring? Your Government extended the intervention in south Wales, where commissioners set the levy paid by local councils. Will your draft budget fund those improvements so councils are not left to meet the additional costs? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Steve Bayliss', '', '8696', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Statement by the First Minister: Weather, wildfires, drought and resilience', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=8447&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'draft budget', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:25.878107', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769180', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('932fcf7c3348432eed7e', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'That''s good to get on the public record. Thank you, Cabinet Minister. Niamh.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6251&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.138618', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772110', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9365dce8a12576804cab', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Before I go into details on the position of Welsh Labour, I want to thank Elin Jones for her engagement with me on the supplementary budget, and I do hope that she knows of the respect that my colleagues and I have for her. I suspect that if she''d had more freedom to negotiate we wouldn''t be in the situation that we''re in today.

 Unfortunately, I fear that there are others in Plaid Cymru who thought that they didn''t need to negotiate, or they preferred to do that through attacking videos on Facebook and briefings to the media. In looking to the process for next year''s budget, I do hope that the Government will reflect on how to avoid the disorder seen over the last three weeks.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=23959&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.579761', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767951', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('938e10f0606afa05fe2b', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'No, I haven''t, but I think that that pragmatic approach to circumstances is one that the UK Government should consider. And now that you''ve raised it, I''m sure it''s something that I can do.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2687&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.681480', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771875', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('93d318f4f513c5d01bfc', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'That''s where the roles of returning officers, chief executives, sitting with that dual hat and viewing elections as an important role to be done across a range of areas of the council, are really critical. Obviously, that''s why the complementary work of the AEA and the electoral management board, bringing returning officers together with electoral administrators so that that co-ordination happens effectively and in a high-quality way is really, really critical. As an EMB, we''re only 15 months in, but I think what you''ll see is that best practice rolled out as a result of that effective co-ordination. I think that''s some of the findings from the Electoral Commission''s report on the Senedd elections, that you''re starting to see stronger and more effective co-ordination.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Ian Bancroft', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5822&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.491828', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769960', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('943144ed36a0627f0680', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I think you''ll be a regular visitor to our committee, Cabinet Minister.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=7127&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.277177', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772089', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('94e05d2a01966368df6b', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'I think, as we said, in terms of delivering this, to not have a knock-on consequence in terms of local government elections, you need to have anybody who needs to be added to the register guaranteed to be on the notice of alteration that is published in March, to allow people to know how many electors are in each ward and the ballot paper requirements, and not introduce the risk that exists there. So, it''s knowing that everything that falls back from that, that you can send notification letters out by this point and that the software is ready to do the data matching that allows you to do that.

 I think we''re very close even now of knowing that everything is in place to be able to deliver that with confidence. At the moment, there is no ability for anything to slip, and that''s worrying. Experience from the pilots is that naturally things slip slightly, but while we''re still doing this, to expect someone to implement it before the local government elections next year is definitely a scary prospect, I think, that a lot of administrators are finding themselves facing.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6345&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.582417', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769956', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('94ec981265786bee928c', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Yes, common land does bring a different kind of challenge, doesn''t it? It always does, it always has done, because of, as you say, the large numbers of interested parties, and it is a challenge, sometimes, to bring them together in order to bring schemes or bring opportunities forward, but we are keen, again, to be flexible in that space, and optional schemes are ones that we want to encourage those particular kinds of farmers to be involved in. I don''t know if you want to add about the habitat.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1497&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.479226', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771812', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9531135527293b2fbd23', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thanks for that. And I just want to explore some of those, because I think there are several bodies related to the whole election process who can acknowledge that a reduction in that time frame might be advantageous in some ways, but then there are serious concerns being voiced by the same people about those who might be more vulnerable, disabled, in circumstances where they''re threatened by domestic abuse, and they don''t want their data being put out. And so there are some real issues around safeguards around this, and some anxiety from One Voice Wales, for instance, and our local authorities, who can see both sides of the coin. And we know that the Electoral Reform Society also believes that, whilst we might need some change, we need really strong safeguards around that. So, where are you on that? And what engagement has been had with those harder-to-reach communities on the ones that are taxing people a little with regard to safeguarding?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=859&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.818723', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769797', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9581d219d1d2c656c240', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Diolch, Lywydd. The issue of parking at the Maelor hospital in Wrexham is raised with us time and time again, and some residents have told us that they''ve arrived sometimes up to three hours before an appointment just to find a parking space. And that will be contributing to the 27,000 missed appointments at the Maelor last year. We''ve raised that repeatedly with the health board, alongside other local Members as well, the council and residents, too, yet there are still no concrete plans to tackle the problem. My own mother is currently undergoing cancer treatment, so I''ve seen first-hand the extra stress that that causes for people who are already worried about their health. I welcome the Government''s commitment in the supplementary budget to boost funding for the NHS, and I also hope that this budget passes to allow that support to happen. First Minister, do you agree that accessing healthcare should begin at the hospital entrance and not with a queue for a parking space? And what discussions is the Welsh Government having with Betsi Cadwaladr to ensure that this long-running problem is finally treated as a priority?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Carrie Harper', '', '278', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=657&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.241740', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767466', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('95b6dafbb33d90d93022', 'press_release', 'Welsh Government — Announcement', 'Statement from the Deputy First Minister: Visit with the Prime Minister', 'Statement from the Deputy First Minister: Visit with the Prime Minister
The Deputy First Minister, Sioned Williams, has issued the following statement.

The Deputy First Minister, Sioned Williams, has issued the following statement.

Deputy First Minister, Sioned Williams, said:
“I’m pleased to meet the Prime Minister today. It is a positive example of governments working together to improve people''s lives. Co-operation on energy and industrial strategy is essential if Wales is to seize the opportunities of the green transition and create well-paid jobs for the future.
“With the energy price cap set to rise by a further 4% ahead of winter, households are coming under renewed pressure. That is why the Welsh Government is calling on the UK Government to introduce an energy social tariff to support those facing fuel poverty and struggling with high energy bills.
“Households in Wales face some of the highest energy standing charges in the UK, adding to the burden on families already under pressure. We are calling on the UK Government to address this longstanding unfairness. Consideration must also be given to supporting the many households in rural Wales that rely on costly heating oil because they are not connected to the mains gas grid.
“Wales is an energy-rich nation. People should see that reflected in good jobs, stronger communities and lower bills.”
ENDS', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/statement-from-the-deputy-first-minister-visit-with-the-prime-minister', '2026-08-26', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Enterprise; Energy; Deputy First Minister; Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality - Sioned Williams', '', '', NULL, 49.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'energy_efficiency', 'Property & energy', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'fuel poverty', 0, '2026-09-11T12:16:42.909754', 'newsroom:statement-from-the-deputy-first-minister-visit-with-the-prime-minister', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('95b6e5169c59ad45cd99', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Well, we do legislate bilingually, of course, and the resources are available to ensure that people know that the Welsh language version is at the same level as the English version, and that they''re available. Probably the most important thing is that we go public with all of this to ensure that people—. We don''t have to spend a great deal of money to do it, but that we take every opportunity to say, ''Well, look, this legislation is bilingual. If you want to turn to the Welsh language, do so, you''re more than welcome to do so; there''s the same authority behind the Welsh version as the English version and they''re equal.'' That''s an important message because even to this day, I know that people believe, for some reason, that there isn''t the same authority behind the Welsh version as there is for the English version. It''s hard to believe, but that''s what people think. So, it''s up to us, at every opportunity, to explain, ''Well, we are a bilingual legislature, the two languages have equal status and so, if you wish to use the Welsh language, please do so.'' In the same way, the use of the Welsh language in the courts, that''s part of my role as well. Sorry—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4486&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.876197', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770580', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('95d44d8a32c4db7a0138', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Good morning—oh, good afternoon. We''ve heard a lot about capacity already, so I won''t dwell too much on it, but it''s fundamental for us to get underneath the capacity question, really. So, I would like to know how the Government is preparing to meet that capacity shortfall, if you like, because there seems to be a bit of ambiguity about capacity—we might have enough, we''re going to look for more, you know. So, what firm steps are you taking to firm up capacity, and do you believe, then, that you will have the capacity to deliver against your key priorities that are coming forward in legislation?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2358&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.483409', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770446', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('970309428a96b4f62972', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '''Planning Policy Wales''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning policy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.156856', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768140', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('971208620eb103de5884', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', 'Thank you. I welcome the Cabinet Minister''s announcement of a 30 per cent cut to business rates for hospitality and leisure businesses. This will be a vital lifeline to small hospitality and leisure businesses and has already been welcomed by businesses in Ceredigion Penfro. These small businesses play essential roles in our rural and coastal communities, protecting local jobs, strengthening local economies and building lasting prosperity. I''m happy to see that this announcement extends beyond just pubs, clubs and live music venues, like in England, to support visitor accommodation and leisure businesses like hotels, cinemas, theatres, museums and gyms. With this in mind, can the Minister explain what she anticipates now will be the practical impact of these measures for towns and high streets across Wales, as well as in our constituency of Ceredigion Penfro?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Anna Nicholl', '', '12178', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=5515&l=en', NULL, 99.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates; visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:17.221935', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769623', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97214416c8e384252aec', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yes, just to update. The volunteering policy existed in 2015. In the last couple of years, there was a voluntary sector cross-sector leadership group that looked at that volunteering policy. It concluded that it needed to be updated, and so, last year, ''A new approach to volunteering'' was published. The conversation we''re having now is around what does that look like over this Senedd term, given the Ministers'' and the Government''s commitments. And as the Deputy First Minister has said, that conversation is happening with Cabinet shortly and with officials. But I just want to put on the record the recognition of that work.

 I think you''re right; volunteering has changed significantly over those 10 years—very different pressures on volunteers—and therefore the response from Government and the sector needs to change. And that has been a really important part of this work, working with the county voluntary councils, which are actually a really important part of the third sector and volunteering infrastructure in Wales.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Andrew Charles', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=7628&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.376214', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771752', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97504ee4f4c01ecdcedb', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question (tabled)', 'OQ64597', 'What assessment has the Cabinet Minister made of how the rights of children are protected when families are placed in temporary accommodation?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OrderPaper/OralQuestions/30-09-2026/', '2026-09-23', 'Lyn Ackerman', '', '', '', 'Casnewydd Islwyn', 'Plenary', 'To the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice and Equality', '', '', '2026-09-30', 45.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'homelessness', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'temporary accommodation', 0, '2026-09-24T06:33:44.167231', 'orderpaper:2026-09-30:OQ64597', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97520a327c8825f75c5d', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'As my colleague Huw Thomas said, the Labour Members will be voting against the supplementary budget today. I want to be clear that I take absolutely no pleasure in this situation. This has always been and will always be about getting more funding for children and young people with ALN in Wales.

 I respectfully remind all the Plaid Cymru backbenchers who have made videos and statements over the weekend attacking Labour for our stance that there was not one single penny for additional learning needs in the supplementary budget until we in the Labour group started pushing for it. Not a single penny. As a result of the discussions this week, the Plaid Cymru minority Government has offered just £40 million for ALN this year, just 12 per cent of the consequential received as a result of the SEND announcement in England. Our view is that that is not enough.

 Yes, we need to invest in the transformation of ALN. That was one of the main findings of the ALN legislative review I commissioned and was something I was working on before the election. But we cannot do that without acknowledging and addressing the massive pressures that are in the system now, today. It is disingenuous of the Trefnydd, the First Minister and other Plaid Cymru Members to say that this was more than the money we put on the table, because the ALN consequentials were only announced in March, after the final budget was passed. So, it could not be allocated without a supplementary budget. Notwithstanding that, I''ve already told the Chamber that I asked for £100 million to be earmarked for ALN. That was the advice received from officials, the amount that we could spend in year.

 It''s not just Welsh Labour who don''t think £40 million is enough; every local authority of every political colour has called for more funding for ALN. And now we''ve heard from our trade union partners. The National Education Union has said they welcome the additional funding, but, based on their evidence from members, this doesn''t go far enough and more money needs to be spent. As I highlighted earlier, the National Association of Head Teachers and the Association of School and College Leaders have taken the unprecedented step of declaring a trade dispute with Welsh Government, citing ALN investment as one of the reasons. They are well aware of the latest offer from the Welsh Government. It is shocking that so early in this Senedd term the Government are in dispute with school leaders in Wales.

 Now, I recognise that Plaid Cymru won the election and are the biggest party, but you are still a minority party, and the way you have conducted some of the negotiations with my party does not bode well for the rest of this Senedd term. There have been some constructive discussions, but there have also been ultimata and negotiations conducted through the media and social media. I really wanted to get to a position where we could support your budget. Just this afternoon, we have asked the Government to withdraw the budget so we can continue negotiations and enable the Government to address the concerns of the teaching unions. If this budget does not pass today, it will be because of your intransigence.

 The finance Minister—[ Interruption .] The finance Minister has told the Chamber today that even if the budget doesn''t pass, you will still be able to spend the money on your priorities, but that you won''t spend money on ALN. Now, that will be your choice and, if I can say so, it will be an act of supreme spite.

 I have heard the heckles all afternoon from this Government that we are voting with Reform. That is beneath you on an issue of this importance, and it''s a sign that you cannot win the argument. So, I want to be clear: we are not voting with Reform, we are not voting with the Welsh Conservatives; we are voting with children and young people with additional learning needs, and we are voting for the school staff who support them, in the belief that those children and those school staff deserve more from this Plaid Cymru Government.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Lynne Neagle', '', '174', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=25074&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'final budget; supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.706456', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767900', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97a575195032da7786be', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Yes, I think we''re pleased that the Welsh Government are addressing all these issues. They have a working group that they have set up and they are taking feedback and information from electoral registration officers. They''re responding to those concerns. I think the primary driver for the concern is around the timescales for the legislation and then the delivery of the changes to the electoral management software systems. So, I think if there were to be any delay to the regulations, then I think that''s where the most significant challenge to delivery for the May 2027 elections would come.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=440&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session; working group', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.740692', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769825', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97a65ac953be1259ba3e', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'Yes, you''ve got to give me time.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26310&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.844239', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768000', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97a7c3b450eab015b29c', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'It''s a change to be on this side of the table, Chair.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', 'Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=61&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.274279', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771837', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('97b06da7d113d08f0967', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'I think that, in terms of the benefits, if this is to be rolled out before the local government elections next year, you have to have a 28-day period. You''ve run out of time to have a 60-day period without it impacting detrimentally on the delivery of the local government elections next year. With a 60-day window, the earliest we''re looking at notification letters going out—. Given that we haven''t got legislation in place yet, the EMS suppliers haven''t got what they need, the Electoral Commission have had to produce guidance before the legislation has come out, again, we''re in a position where January, early January is the absolute earliest we can look at notification letters going out. A 60-day window would then push you into those people being added to the register during the notice of election period. So, you wouldn''t have poll cards for those people until later, which, again, if we''re introducing people to a democratic process and then putting barriers in their way or sort of not giving them a good first experience is detrimental there. So, having a 28-day window in this case to allow you to have those people on the register by the 1 March notice of alteration is crucial to make sure that they''re included in the data that electoral administrators will send to printers to administer that election.

 There are obviously drawbacks, I guess, in that, if you''ve got anonymous electors or if you''ve got ineligible electors who''ve unintentionally been sent correspondence—. A longer period obviously gives people more time to respond to that. But I think that, in terms of what the pilots showed, most people tended to respond within a two to three-week period in that window, so that 28-day period would be sufficient. I think Sandra''s experience, which I''m sure she can elaborate on as well, is that when they sent reminders during that 60-day window, because it allowed for that to happen, you sometimes still had people responding to it, but generally speaking, I think the pilots have shown that those risks were relatively minimal and a 28-day window is sufficient.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4496&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.270410', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769911', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9826ecbc74fe619a9373', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', 'Item 8. Legislative Consent Motion: Steel Industry (Nationalisation) Bill: For: 86, Against: 7, Abstain: 0

 Motion has been agreed

 Click to see vote results', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '10. Voting Time', '16085', '', NULL, 16.5, 'Noise', 'archive', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', '', '', 'Legislative Consent Motion', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.867070', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767986', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('988ac88aa6b6c262c8b2', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'As I said, because of the engagement work that''s currently under way and the refining process for the strategy, we will be able to bring forward the new strategy and circulate that for consultation early on next year, in 2027.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=758&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Consultation open', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; consultation', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.636565', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771201', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('989c2d6d235ba58c348b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Yes. I''ll refer Members, first of all, though, to my declaration of interest as a shareholder in a company renting out a domestic property.

 Strengthening the private rented sector is a priority for this Government; I think we''ve made that pretty clear, we set it out in my legislative statement on 14 July. Renters in Wales deserve a safe, warm, secure and affordable home and should feel confident challenging poor conditions. Positive change requires good law, and in the first phase of our legislative programme, we''ll deliver legislation that protects tenants and tackles poor landlords, making renting, hopefully, more secure.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=2155&l=en', NULL, 82.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'private_rented_sector; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'landlords; legislative programme; private rented; private rented sector; renters', 0, '2026-09-16T11:53:07.203077', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769001', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('98bf773262165f23ccfe', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I know John wants to come in, but I''ve got a further question. So, I''ll bring Andrew in, then I''ll come back and I''ll bring John in, if that''s all right, on this point.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1109&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.428607', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771745', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('98ce658fe2caf2a6fc95', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Those are very clear lived challenges that you managed to get around. Do you believe that, if that was rolled out all over, there are answers to those challenges, which can make this truly deliverable? Or would we have a half-cocked process that couldn''t manage?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4090&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.197017', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769926', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('98d2d86a63ab8d1449f8', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'I agree totally with what Sandra said. There are lots of individuals within local authorities who''ve already got the skills for doing the data matching, data mining. Electoral administrators like to think we''re jacks of all trades, but in terms of actually providing that expertise and capacity to do the work, again, not to hark back, but it''s having enough notice to be able to reconfigure your resources to allow you to do that. Again, we''re in a period where I don''t know whether you can suddenly change workload as quickly as that with a month''s notice. I think there are concerns around that aspect as well in terms of that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=6710&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.649575', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770262', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('99344a5f1bd51adac543', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', 'I was obviously too keen to go to the pub to prepare for this statement, so I''ll reflect on what everybody has united upon in thinking about the timing of statements. But just very genuinely from me, I could have made this statement during the summer recess, in the peak of the tourism season, but I quite deliberately left it until the first day back. But, yes, I''ll consider the timing, and getting back in to the rhythm of the work of this place is something that I need to do.

 Yes, just on the issue around a cliff edge, I do recognise that this is a very difficult issue for some businesses who fall the wrong side of a support package of this kind, and only very closely. So, the tapering issue is something that I will be interested in looking at, but I didn''t want to delay the simplicity and the ability to make this statement and to move on this level of support of 30 per cent reduction for the hospitality and leisure sectors in this way by trying to do everything at the same time in one go. So, these are issues that I will be returning to, and they are issues, of course, that the high-street taskforce will also want to look at, I''m sure, as are many of the issues that you raise. The issue around whether new start-ups on the high street should be given a holiday period for that first six months to a year of trading as a kind of incentive for them to take up, quite often, empty shops, making local authorities more proactive in their local area to support business growth as well by incentivising growth of businesses and growth of business rate collection, with some payback back to local authorities—. I''m always interested in how we can make these systems include more incentives to do better all the time and to have more economic activity. So, those are some things that I hope the town-centres taskforce will look at, but also that we do in response to the issues that are raised in the Chamber here today and at other times.

 On mixed businesses, I''ve come across this as an issue in my own constituency at different times as well, so I''m aware that it''s an issue in the system and a tension. I will need to go back to my officials and see whether there is anything in the greater extent of the categorisation that we''re now bringing into the 30 per cent reduction that will enable mixed businesses of the kinds you''ve described to qualify for this. But I can''t give a guarantee on the floor here in any individual case or generally on mixed businesses, but I will look at it to see what is possible.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Elin Jones', '', '162', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10800&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.199011', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769229', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('99c93caa19a018369912', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Diolch, Lywydd, especially for that kind and polite reminder of bringing announcements like this first to the Senedd.

 First Minister, there are many aspects to welcome, I believe, in this statement, but I would appreciate clarity on a number of points. It was of course one of the Government''s key commitments to put rural-proofing on a statutory footing, and I look forward to scrutinising that legislation. I''ll be particularly interested to understand what outcomes it''ll drive for rural communities, because the test, of course, is not the drafting and the passage of the Bill, but what changes for rural communities. Likewise, on the fair rent Bill, I''m proud of how the last Welsh Labour Government strengthened protections for tenants, and my colleagues and I look forward to scrutinising the Government''s proposals in this area.

 I''d particularly like to welcome the Government''s announcement of a new law creating a community right to buy. This is a long-standing priority for many communities across Wales, and is particularly championed by the Co-operative Party. It empowers local people to bring valuable community spaces back into community ownership, and I know that work on this started under the last Welsh Labour Government. Of course, in England there is already a community right to buy, introduced by the UK Labour Government, and I''m a huge advocate of giving communities more power in decision making over their own areas. It''s exactly what the community right to buy helps to achieve—places like The Raven Inn in Llanarmon-yn-Iâl; I believe it''s frequented by Darren Millar many times. That shows just how powerful a community right-to-buy scheme can be in empowering and uniting a community. I think it''s an important tool in helping community groups to buy places like pubs, but also like schools, shops, open spaces and other places in towns and villages, and indeed in our cities. It protects places that make our communities special, that matter to people, and gives people more of a say in designing their own high streets.

 Now, the UK Labour Government''s backed this up with a fund to help communities to buy assets. So, what additional practical support will this Government put in place to ensure that communities can actually make use of the new law? I''d particularly suggest that the community facilities programme could be a useful vehicle for providing financial support and indeed advice to communities that need it.

 The First Minister won''t be surprised to hear me raise legislation for a school staff negotiating body in the Siambr. I''m disappointed to see that it''s not included in the statement today. We committed, when we were in Government, to a school support staff negotiating body, and much of the preparatory work is done. It''s ready to go, in fact. And, of course, when the Senedd recently voted on this matter, there was cross-party support for it. We were always clear, though, in Government that a new negotiating body would have addressing year-round pay as its first priority, because across Wales, 27,000 school support staff—the teaching assistants, the kitchen staff, caretakers and many others that schools rely on—are only being paid during term time. And, of course, we''re mere days away from the school summer holidays, a time at which, at least symbolically, these vital staff members are without pay. And it is about fair pay, it''s about job security, it''s about social justice, it''s about stability for children, and it''s also about equality, because 90 per cent of school support staff are women. For thousands of families across Wales, year-round pay would be a huge step forward, and I would urge the Government to commit to taking this forward as soon as possible.

 Now, another Bill I''d hoped to see in today''s statement was a taxi and private hire vehicles Bill, which, as many Members will know, was developed as a White Paper under the Welsh Labour Government. Such a Bill would legislate to regulate the taxi and private hire vehicle sector, including addressing the problem of cross-bordering, which is particularly prevalent in Cardiff and Newport. Now, since the White Paper, the need for this legislation has only increased. There''s no doubt about it. We now live in an age of rapidly evolving technology, particularly with regard to automation, that will fundamentally change how people across Wales work, how they travel and therefore how they use taxis and private hire vehicles. This legislation, I believe, will only become more relevant and necessary as the pace of change continues to increase, and I''d urge the Government to give this matter close consideration. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Ken Skates', '', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=9312&l=en', NULL, 159.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; social_housing', 'Housing system; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', 'Legislative commitment', 'White Paper; community right to buy; fair rent; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:09.043340', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767755', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9a34dbe1708ce66c7f86', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', 'Diolch, Weinidog, for your commitment to championing a community-centred planning system and strengthening the voice of local people. Cymru is emerging as a significant location for data-centre development, with two designated UK Government AI growth zones. However, they have no dedicated use class and no mention in ''Planning Policy Wales'', even though their scale and energy and water demands raise important questions for our environment and our planning system beyond their immediate locality. As you know, in my own constituency of Caerdydd Ffynnon Taf, a data centre is proposed in Llanrumney, replacing the Cardiff east park and ride, which serves the University Hospital of Wales, and this has given rise to a controversy beyond the immediate locality. The proposed 150 MW facility, only a short distance from primary schools and local homes, will rely on fossil-fuelled gas turbines as well as polluting standby diesel generators. Will the Minister, therefore, set out what plans the Government has to review the planning requirements for data centres, and in particular look into the Cardiff east park-and-ride application, so that communities like Llanrumney don''t end up paying the social and environmental price while the profit goes elsewhere? Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'Nick Carter', '', '12173', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=4999&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'Planning Policy Wales; planning policy; planning system', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:05.173440', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768167', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9a72b6088766a25348d3', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Mine is just jumping back, sorry, to agriculture, if that''s okay.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Niamh Salkeld', '', '12143', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2074&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.586642', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771993', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9a90dd8d855e8871d683', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', 'Whilst I''m grateful to the Government for the advance notice of these legislative priorities issued via the media this morning, surely Government announcements, especially when they relate to Bills to be introduced in this place, should be first made in this place, out of respect for all Members of this Parliament. I would encourage the Government to reflect on that and treat this place with the respect that it deserves.

 The statement refers to this programme as a far-reaching and ambitious programme that reflects the values of the Government. That may be so if the bar is set low. It doesn''t squarely reflect the priorities of the people of Wales. On the cost of living, making rents fairer is a noble aim, but the root cause of unaffordable rents is the housing shortage. The Government should be tackling the root cause of this by overhauling the planning system, giving house builders more certainty and skilling up our young people for a lifetime of work in construction. We should also be giving councils more powers to say ''no'' to houses in multiple occupation.

 The new development agency is an admission that this Government does not possess the expertise to directly deal with business and industry. For a moment there during the First Minister''s speech, I thought that the Minister for enterprise was coming to sit next to me to tell me so. He got a little lost in the Chamber, and I think that''s an omen for how lost the Government is on its policy with the economy. But businesses want to deal with decision makers, not agencies acting as a sales force. They want decisions on planning, on land assembly, on grant funding and business rate incentives. No quango can offer that. Plaid have finally got power and they''re handing it over to yet another quango. Far from creating more quangos, we should be consolidating them and bringing their functions in-house and saving on the extortionate management costs.

 On rural-proofing, as was the case with the sustainable farming scheme, the devil will be in the detail. It sounds like another environmental sustainability scheme, but what rural communities need is the Government off their backs when it comes to food production, and acting fairly when it comes to transport, health and education. Food security and increasing food exports is a large part of rural-proofing. Where is the legislation to safeguard those sectors?

 On tribunals, not one voter in Wales told me they wanted tribunal reform, but I do understand the importance of the tribunal system. But the language used to justify it is all about making the case for further devolution of justice and taking us on Plaid''s journey to independence.

 On water reform, my group tabled a motion to clean up our waters, our seas, our rivers, which Plaid shamefully voted against. Today''s announcement is a welcome u-turn, and I''m pleased that Plaid are now following Reform''s lead on this topic. And there could be room for consensus on this—hopefully so; you talked about consensus, First Minister. 

 Tackling child poverty—sadly, three decades of devolution has done little to eradicate poverty in Wales, and giving free childcare to all families, even those where the parents are millionaires, isn''t going to make the impact the Government expects. I understand the aim is for parents to be able to go out and work, but where are the jobs? Where is the plan for an economic boom in Wales?

 This Government appears to have chosen topics where it thinks there is consensus, and I understand why a minority Government would do that. But another approach would be to unite this Chamber with a more ambitious vision for Wales—a vision that sees Wales lead in certain sectors, such as nuclear energy and logistics, a vision that sees farmers produce even more of our high-quality food to export, to enable a radical step change in house building, and to significantly tighten up the performance management of the health boards, the health chiefs and the education sector.

 Wales has so much potential, but this programme does little to unleash it. We''ve seen these kinds of Bills and measures before. They have made little difference to the daily lives of the people of Wales. People voted for change, but they''ve ended up with continuity. Where Reform can see merit in legislation, we will support, we will offer our own ideas, but this is a missed opportunity to take Wales''s story to another level, and I can''t help but feel that the people of Wales will be underwhelmed when they see this programme as a whole.

 Now, I know much can be achieved without laws and with the normal levers of Government, but we have waited 30 years for this moment. There are just under four years left of this term, and Reform will do all it can to increase the level of ambition in this Parliament. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Dan Thomas', '', '12147', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the First Minister: Legislation', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=8823&l=en', NULL, 214.5, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'rent_controls_and_affordability; hmos_and_standards; housing_supply; planning_system; fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context; Planning & place; Private rented sector', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'house building; houses in multiple occupation; legislative priorities; multiple occupation; planning system; unaffordable rents', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.983663', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767757', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9af045fbfbe0b8740e80', 'oral_question', 'Oral Question', 'Oral Question - OQ64420', 'What action is the Welsh Government taking to meet its social housing targets?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/OralQuestion/64420', '2026-07-08', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', 'Sir Fynwy Torfaen', 'Senedd (tabled business)', 'Tabled on 08/07/2026', '', '', NULL, 40.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'housing_supply; social_housing', 'Housing system; Planning & place', '', '', 'housing targets; social housing', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:42.308411', 'record_search:type=oral_question:query=housing', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9b467eeb251857166257', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'Minister, the focus in this Chamber thus far has been on the cost of childcare plans, rather than on recognising childcare as a critical lever for reducing both poverty and inequality. Childcare is a complicated issue—just ask any parent attempting to fill out an eligibility form—but it''s essential economic and social infrastructure. And this is where we, across the political spectrum, must scrutinise your work. This policy will reduce child poverty and support women who''ve been forced out of the workplace by the motherhood penalty, which impacts everything from your pay packet to your pension. We saw on the doorstep how many people cared about this issue, which is why they voted for Plaid Cymru in their droves.

 The UK Labour Government took their eye off the ball, which is why we''ve ended up with the most expensive childcare, here in Wales, across the UK, and why parents have said to me that they''re moving out of Wales simply to make ends meet. Twenty-seven million pounds was reallocated out of the childcare budget in the last term, along with £140 million in Barnett consequentials that were lost due to the expansion of childcare in England. They haven''t been spent on childcare. Credit has to be given to Jane Dodds, who secured £30 million for Flying Start expansion. That helped support the roll-out of Flying Start in Merthyr. But I wish to ask why you, Minister, have made childcare a key priority for this Plaid Cymru Government, and, specifically, given that money that has been stripped from the childcare budget in the last term, what concrete milestones you have, particularly in the next six months, to ensure that we''re not just planning reform, but actively reversing the damage done to Welsh families.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Sarah Rees', '', '5196', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=153&l=en', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'planning reform', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.452427', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766806', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9b913565f4c6f2a6baa5', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', 'Cabinet Minister, thank you for your statement today. I do share some of the concerns raised by our Reform colleague in terms of the way in which this information has been disseminated to Members of the Senedd. I think it is right and proper that in the vast majority of instances, information is shared in this Chamber first before being released to the press, just to show some respect to the Chamber itself and the Members within it.

 But in terms of the meat of this statement, I certainly welcome the direction of travel from the Government on this and recognise that business rates represent a significant cost for businesses across Wales at a time when many are already under considerable pressures. Cabinet Minister, you''ll be aware that our high streets have been struggling to adapt to changing consumer behaviour and the continued growth of online retail. The Welsh Conservatives have consistently argued that the tax burden on those businesses is far too high. We also need to be honest about the wider pressures that businesses are facing, dealing with taxation of all types alongside employee pressures and rising energy costs. Of course, business rates are only one part of a wider cost of doing business.

 Colleagues have already raised a couple of questions I was interested in. I will point to three issues and questions, though. Respectfully, Cabinet Minister, I''m not sure if you fully responded to Huw Thomas''s question, particularly around the cliff edge. You acknowledged it in your response, but, of course, there has potentially been the introduction of a new cliff edge here. I wonder what assurances you can give those businesses that they won''t feel the pain of that, potentially, and that there may be some tapering that could take place so businesses aren''t falling foul one way or the other. What confidence can you give to businesses that that is properly understood and recognised in terms of a potential tapering of this relief beyond that £51,000? A broader discussion you mentioned in regard to business rates, I think, does need to take place. I''d expect Welsh Government to lead on that.

 There was one specific issue I''d be grateful for you to consider in this discussion, and that''s one around the particular challenges faced by businesses who are just starting out. Business rates will ordinarily apply as soon as they start up, meaning firms can face a tax bill very soon after opening their doors without necessarily generating real income at that point. I wonder what consideration you might give to our proposal for business rate free periods for new retail businesses and others on the high street to give those entrepreneurs the opportunity to establish themselves before facing this additional cost.

 Another consideration on which I''d be interested in your response is the role of local authorities in business rates. Of course, many people think that councils set those business rates, but they merely collect them on behalf of the Welsh Government. But I wonder if there''s an opportunity to incentivise our local authorities by allowing them to retain an element of any non-domestic rates growth in their area, to allow our councils to invest in our town centres or incentivise our councils perhaps to invest in those town centres more than they currently do.

 Finally, Minister, I''m interested—and it''s back to this particular announcement today—in how it''s going to impact businesses that offer mixed services. In my own constituency, Banana Ben''s play centre and cafe may be impacted by this. They''ve been refused food and drink hospitality rate relief in the past, despite food and drink accounting for 60 per cent of their floor space and 61 per cent of their turnover. Companies House classify it as an unlicensed restaurant and cafe. The business provides indoor play alongside food and drink for families, and the two, of course, are not mutually exclusive, yet it will not, it seems, qualify for the equivalent relief. Businesses of a similar nature in England absolutely do. So, I wonder whether you could respond to that and what you might seek to do to address this disparity to ensure that Welsh businesses are not disadvantaged simply because they operate in Wales, perhaps offering more than one service to their customers. Diolch yn fawr iawn.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Sam Rowlands', '', '8734', '', '', 'Plenary', '4. Statement by the Cabinet Minister for Finance: Rebalancing the non-domestic rates system', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=10518&l=en', NULL, 71.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'business rates; non-domestic rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:26.156303', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769216', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9c0a8322c3c801e9a7be', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'I''ve got a point to make about what you''ve just said, which is that we''re not supposed to be here as Members of parties, we''re supposed to be here to scrutinise the Minister. So, I''d respectfully just ask you to think about that and not refer to your political party or conferences. Thank you.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=2023&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.781870', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771176', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9c2fcc66aaf420bc405f', 'consultation', 'Senedd — committee consultation', 'Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', 'Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee

This is an open Senedd consultation. Responding puts NRLA''s position formally on the record.

Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee, start date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 00:00:00 GMT, end date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:59:00 GMT

Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Priorities for the Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee View the background to this consultation View all current consultations Purpose of the consultation The L ocal Government, Housing and Planning Committee has been set up by the Senedd to look at policy and legislation, and to hold the Welsh Government to account on issues relevant to its remit. To help inform its planning, the Committee is seeking views from people and organisations on the top three issues they believe it should prioritise in the Seventh Senedd. To submit your views, please complete the online form . The closing date for sharing your views is 14 September 2026 . Providing Written Evidence The Senedd has two official languages, Welsh and English. In line with the Senedd’s Official Languages Scheme , the Committee welcomes contributions in both or either of our official languages, and we ask organisations that are subject to Welsh Language standards or schemes to respond in line with their own obligations. Please inform the Committee when submitting responses if you intend to provide a translation at a later date. Please see guidance for those providing evidence for committees . Disclosure of information Please ensure that you have considered the Senedd’s policy on disclosure of information before submitting information to the Committee. Contact details Should you wish to speak to someone regarding this consultation, please use the below contact details: Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee Welsh Parliament Cardiff Bay Cardiff CF99 1SN Email: SeneddHousing@senedd.wales Telephone: 0300 200 6565 Topics Helpful Links Find a Member of the Senedd chevron_right Sign up to updates from Senedd committees chevron_right Senedd Now chevron_right Glossary chevron_right Related No results were found

Closing date: 14 September 2026.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgConsultationDisplay.aspx?ID=626', '2026-07-17', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd committee', '', '', '', '2026-09-14', 224.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'NRLA named', 'Consultation open; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; closing date; consultation; consultations; seeking views; written evidence', 1, '2026-08-04T14:02:35.091543', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgRss.aspx?f=76', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9c8732353c5961a797c3', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yes. I think there''s a lot, because, under the legislation, prevention is one of the key requirements for setting a new strategy. So, a lot of the conversations we''re having now—. The importance of prevention came up quite strongly in the review conversations back in July, so that''s going to be feeding into the new strategy. But a lot of the conversations we''re having now are across Government. We''re starting to talk to colleagues around what they can do to tackle this agenda and what role they can play. But prevention is a really key part both of the commitment in the programme for government and also a key part in the legislation. As the Deputy First Minister said, that strong theme around supporting men and boys, and the Sound campaign, is a really important part of that approach.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Andrew Charles', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5797&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee; programme for government', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.157787', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771292', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('9ca47421dd3ab063a155', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you. The final topic we''ll be going on to is the voluntary sector. Just to check now, would you be able to stay just a couple more minutes?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6787&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.272674', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771250', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
