Meeting status: Confirmed; Hybrid
This is an opportunity to influence: committee papers and any call for written evidence are normally published in the two weeks beforehand.', 'https://business.senedd.wales/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=986&MId=16253', '2026-09-14', '', '', '', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '', '16253', '', '2026-09-14', 48.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; written evidence', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:40.392888', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgWebService.asmx#GetAllMeetingsByDate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bac284c0a08ac6d4c0db', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '—about beavers and their role in restoration over the coming years, and perhaps when you''re looking at those higher tiers in the SFS and some of the things that have already been talked about around rewetting and helping farmers to protect their water courses, I''d love to see that as a long-term goal, tying in with beaver reintroduction.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1935&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.548304', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772016', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bb1337a75a8d836ef452', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', 'Cai has made reference to some of the commentary around the pressures facing Welsh Government in its next budget. The IFS, the Institute for Fiscal Studies, warned in the election campaign about the extremely challenging fiscal outlook facing the next Government, and yet your own party leader assured voters that Plaid Cymru had a fully costed programme for government. It does start to feel now like these were simply making promises without preparing for the consequences. I don''t want to repeat Cai''s question, so let me ask, therefore: if the Government faces a choice between funding its manifesto promises or making cuts to core public services and projects, how will it decide?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=4082&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:17.065954', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769565', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bbe63adbab3326e633d4', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I think, fundamentally, it comes back to the answer I gave some moments ago—and Sophie—around that careful planning, timetabling, working through what needs to be done when, and, when legislation is introduced, what level of scrutiny will be required over a simple, straightforward, non-contentious Bill and over Bills that may be a little more contentious and complex. So, those are precisely the kind of things that we''ve—both the Counsel General and I—engaged with officials in discussions on, and I know there have been—. We met with the former Chair of this committee and discussed some of those matters too face to face. So, I think I''m relatively confident that those lessons have been heeded and that there will be better timetabling of legislation.

 One other point I''d make is that there are certain things that are within our gift. The choice of the UK Government about when to produce legislation and how effectively they''ve co-operated with us in terms of their timetables is another matter, which I have little ability to influence wholly at the moment. Again, I have raised some of these issues with Hamish Falconer, my opposite number in the Cabinet Office in the UK Government, and there is at least an acknowledgement of the issues and a stated desire to work together. So, I hope that''ll be reflected in practice too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1009&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.215061', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770413', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bc5fd735d68689e1461b', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', 'Cabinet Minister, yesterday''s announcement of a 30 per cent cut in business rates for some of the smallest hospitality accommodation and leisure venues is certainly a welcome step forward. However, there is plenty more that the Welsh Government can do to support the tourism industry in Ceredigion Penfro, including reducing the devastating 182-day occupancy threshold for self-catering businesses. Now, I appreciate that the Welsh Government''s consulting on this, but the consultation states that there will be a limit to the extent of any reduction in the threshold, arguing that a substantial decrease in the threshold could incentivise an oversupply of self-catering properties. In light of this, can you clarify what the Welsh Government considers to be the minimum viable occupancy threshold? And in the interests of transparency, will you commit to publishing the evidence underpinning that position, so that business owners can understand the parameters that the Welsh Government is considering in this important consultation?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16261', '2026-09-16', 'Paul Davies', '', '145', '', '', 'Plenary', '2. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Finance', '16261', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16261?startPos=3449&l=en', NULL, 143.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Consultation open', 'business rates; consultation; self-catering', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:16.983848', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16261&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769538', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bc694fd3cc5de8898482', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'I''m grateful for the question. I would respectfully ask the Member to reflect on the code of conduct on pursuing our own particular cases within the Welsh Parliament. But, of course, it is right that we all represent people who have been waiting a very long time on waiting lists. It is the length of waiting lists in terms of numbers and it is the length of time that people are waiting on them that have led this Government to conclude, even before we came into Government, that this was a priority. It''s why we prioritised in the last budget, for 2026-27, the need to invest in the NHS now. It''s why decisions have been made through the supplementary budget, which we hope can be passed today, in order to spend on bringing down waiting times and making the system sustainable, because it is unacceptable. Wherever people are—and that, of course, includes people here in this Siambr who are on waiting lists—it''s not acceptable that we believe this to be normal, and that is why we are placing the utmost seriousness on the work of working with health boards, making the investment, having those performance reviews so that we can build a sustainable NHS and care system for the future.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=563&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.229423', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767497', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bc946c1795c585aac221', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', 'I can predict what you''re going to say now, but just let me read what your leader said in March 2025 in response to the then finance Minister, Mark Drakeford.

 ''The Cabinet Secretary is suggesting that by voting against a flawed budget as a whole, Plaid Cymru is voting against individual spending decisions.''

 ''this is a spurious argument.''

 ''What we''re saying today is that whilst we can agree with elements of the budget...this is a flawed Labour budget and that is why we''ll vote against it today.''', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Ken Skates', '', '267', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Debate: The First Supplementary Budget 2026-27', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=26127&l=en', NULL, 44.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Opposition housing leads', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:10.820980', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767985', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd003855a21915be0ce4', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'Small businesses are the backbone of our local economy in Bangor Conwy Môn. A clear example of this in Bangor is Antoniazzi''s cafe, which has been a prominent presence on the high street for 92 years now. In recent conversations with a number of businesses recently, the same request often rose, namely a fair deal on business rates, and I look forward to hearing the finance Minister''s statement later on this afternoon about that. But does the First Minister agree with me that the Plaid Cymru Government’s commitment to adjust business rates in favour of our independent small businesses will give them the confidence to grow and invest, breathing new life into our town centres in the process?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Mair Rowlands', '', '5084', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=573&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:24.909362', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768989', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd0ab2864d6e15ba2455', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '3. Early business and forward work programme: committee''s remit and responsibilities', 'Moving on, then, to item 3. So, the next item outlines the committee''s remit and responsibilities. If members have detailed questions about the remit, it may be more appropriate to discuss these in more detail during the session later on, in private. But, for now, are members content to note the remit? Yes, happy.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16163', '2026-07-08', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '3. Early business and forward work programme: committee''s remit and responsibilities', '16163', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16163?startPos=84&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:18.014123', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16163&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766763', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd1e17dcbe6c16b5e6aa', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'How can the Welsh Government ensure that this isn''t a Bill for a Bill''s sake, and it will actually make a tangible difference to people''s lives—rather than being top-down, more bottom-up? What types of differences do you think it will make to people''s day-to-day lives?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sarah Rees', '', '5196', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3009&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.891652', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771238', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd33df7373a74c88f566', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Well, there have been examples of errors in the Welsh language versions in the past, haven''t there? So, how you will, as a Welsh Government, now seek to collaborate with Westminster to enable composite and joint statutory instruments to be drawn up in a bilingual format, to try to avoid those kinds of—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Beca Brown', '', '12186', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4847&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.961728', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770613', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bd6291c7d44eeaa9f90b', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Minister speak, yes.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4408&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.865752', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771854', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bdb29dff0333c93fcd75', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Okay. A last quick question on that. So, as the equality Minister, you are going to take responsibility to ensure that all Ministers across all portfolios are adhering to that law and adapting, for example, NHS policy in Wales accordingly.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3435&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.949895', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771208', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bdbeb34c6244e165df0f', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'I would say that overall we do think it''s still possible to roll out automatic registration in time for next year''s local government elections. But I think we would say that it is at the point now where if the timescales slip any further, it''s going to become increasingly challenging to deliver those. I think, speaking frankly, the legislation and the associated implementation activities are being delivered at a later stage than we would ideally expect.

 We would normally look to see legislation clear at least six months before registration officers have to deliver new processes. Obviously, this hasn''t been met on this occasion. It will put additional pressure on those electoral services teams. The critical factor here is the timing of when the regulations will come into effect, because there are activities that registration officers can do, and they are doing, to prepare, but they won''t be able to actually press ''go'' and start that activity until the regulations are passed and in place. We''ve provided some guidance, as I said, at risk based on the draft regulations, but they can''t take action until those regulations are in place.

 On your second question, yes, I think the pilots have been very helpful, our evaluation, but I think probably more importantly the direct experience of some of the local authorities who are involved in the pilots has been really critical in shaping and helping other local authorities understand what it would mean in practice to deliver the new system.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Tom Hawthorn', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=2715&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Legislative commitment; Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; draft regulations; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.108359', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769927', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bde9dbc6f278fea26146', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'I brought something up at First Minister''s questions, but I didn''t receive an answer that I was satisfied with, so I just wanted to raise it with you again as it falls under your purview. There''s a scheme called the ethnic minority initial teacher education scheme, and that pays £5,000 to ethnic minority students who want to become teachers. Now, that money isn''t available to them if they have the wrong skin colour. So, if they''re white, then they can''t have that money. Now, that plainly, obviously, discriminates according to skin colour. It''s by definition, therefore, racist. Now, I understand that you want a level playing field for people regardless of their skin colour, but that''s being achieved—if it is being achieved—with racial discrimination. So, I don''t know why this Government thinks it''s okay to discriminate in accordance to skin colour. And would you be able to shed some light as to why you think that''s okay?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Joe Martin', '', '12146', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4189&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.974881', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771233', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('be1f8ad708212d633574', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'John has been waiting very patiently online. Sorry, John, I''ll bring you in now.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1389&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.466999', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771766', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('be3d0ad3804e2c75780a', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Well, Mr Mason, I''m not so much of a political animal anymore.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1863&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.362434', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770390', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('be6493ddd9030d7d297c', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'I think in terms of what Sandra''s outlined, obviously it''s Powys''s experience, but the implementation of the pilot was over quite a long period of time, and I think our concerns on the manual process—and, as we said, there''s nothing automatic, from an administrator''s point of view, about automatic registration—are the amount of time it takes to manually cleanse the data before you do the data match. Then there''s the manual quality assurance process—the time that takes and the window that we''re looking at in doing that, and the fact that administrators in other authorities, who haven''t had the experience of the pilots, were in a very condensed time frame. So, while some risks can be mitigated in terms of that, I think the biggest risk we''ve got is this short time frame we''ve got to be able to do that in the other 19 authorities that haven''t had the experience that Sandra and Powys have had in that respect.

 I think, in terms of the reliance on manual systems, when a human is responsible for doing those data checks, there''s always that element of risk and error and things as well. And I think our biggest concern is that, if this process appears to be rushed in any way, shape or form, or sufficient time is not given to allow for people to do the work correctly, there''s the risk of adding people incorrectly to the register, because those checks haven''t been done fully or properly. It''s the impact that that has long term on the integrity of the process and on the delivery of elections going forward as well.

 And I think, again, it''s also those concerns around the access to information as well. We''re very much aware that a lot of authorities in Wales are currently struggling to access the local data sources. They need to do this exercise. There are regulations, but the regulations that are relied upon that allow electoral registration officers access to things have always been a barrier. UK Government have recognised this and they''re currently, in the Representation of the People Bill, trying to address that to make sure those barriers don''t exist. And it''s a shame that Welsh Government haven''t carried out those things first to allow greater access to those data sources to take away some of those barriers that currently exist as well. So, again, it''s about having that time period to make sure that the legislative framework and the digital framework are in place to successfully allow for what could be a really successful initiative to get more people on the register and to exercise their democratic rights.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Clare Sim', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=4224&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.219376', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769932', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('beb01229b45d7ac65a52', 'legislation', 'Senedd Act', 'Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026', 'Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026
Current stage: Royal Assent (27 April 2026)
Stage history: Bill introduced 03 Nov 2025; Stage 1 13 Jan 2026; Stage 2 05 Feb 2026; Stage 3 10 Mar 2026; Stage 4 17 Mar 2026; Royal Assent 27 Apr 2026', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46599', '2026-04-27', '', '', '', '', '', 'Senedd', '', '', '', NULL, 130.0, 'Critical', 'immediate', 'second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place', '', 'Bill progress', 'Development of Tourism; Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act; Regulation of Visitor Accommodation; Royal Assent; Stage 1; Stage 2; Stage 3; Stage 4; visitor accommodation', 0, '2026-08-04T14:04:18.484612', 'https://business.senedd.wales/mgIssueHistoryHome.aspx?IId=46599', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('beb25cb5e091da11b205', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Jane Dodds.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5587&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.136236', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771256', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('beb638879a2653889158', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I think the short answer to that, Mr Thomas, is this: we need better co-operation, and, in doing that, we need to engage thoroughly with our colleagues in London at an early stage. There have been unfortunate examples quite recently—they''re truly unfortunate examples—the steel Bill being one, where matters went ahead without even referencing Wales, in effect. That''s not good enough. But there are good examples as well. For example, the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs are renowned for their co-operation. We need to build on that kind of co-operation, so that, early on, we will know whether or not we want to buy in, and, if so, how far and so on, and not have it given to us at the eleventh hour. And that''s really been happening too often in the past, and it''s happened once or twice in the last couple of months as well. It''s not good enough. So, the answer is to spend time in developing good working relationships with our opposite numbers in London. I think that''s the key to this.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=3485&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.687816', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770447', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bebac7e701922937fb59', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'That''s a question I need to think about, really. If they''re not vaccinating, then there is a question about the sustainability of their operations, really, because the risk inherent is that you lose the vast majority, potentially, of your livestock. Now, I don''t think that makes business sense. I think, as the chief vet said, it''s ultimately a commercial decision and it''s one that farmers have to make on their own merits, really, in terms of their particular situation. And if it isn''t bluetongue, it''ll be something else coming behind it. There is that, and we''re in this world where we have to recalibrate a little bit the way that we consider how we run our businesses and how we look after our livestock because it''s going to be one challenge after another. So, I''ll need to reflect a bit on that question. But, certainly, it''s this journey that we''re on, isn''t it?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=6546&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.190068', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#772079', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bf17f1739de16c166eae', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thank you. Elyn, can we go back to your question?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Carmelo Colasanto', '', '12152', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1950&l=en', NULL, 103.5, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'LGHP Committee members', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.982311', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769850', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bf27124633884002fcb7', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I wouldn''t want anyone to comment on any other parts of any of the Government portfolios, Minister; you''ve only just come into post.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4940&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.955223', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771806', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bf57f0f9196a9293f665', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '8. Voting Time', 'Motion NDM9284 as amended:

 To propose that the Senedd: 

 1. Recognises the vital contribution of the tourism sector to the Welsh economy, directly supporting more than 150,000 jobs and contributing to 5 per cent of Wales''s gross value added. 

 2. Celebrates Wales as a world-class destination, welcoming visitors from across the globe. 

 3. Welcomes the Welsh Government’s support for the tourism sector by:

 a) promoting the visitor levy as a choice for local authorities, that can enable new investment into local tourism; and

 b) delivering the Government’s commitment to keep the 182-day occupancy threshold for self-catering business under review and create clear and reasonable exemptions where a property would not qualify as a private home.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', '', '', '', '', '', 'Plenary', '8. Voting Time', '16084', '', NULL, 77.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets', 'Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'self-catering; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.141412', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767138', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bf66f35cab056caf6e29', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'And, of course, what is mentioned now has changed in a revolutionary manner since the late Dr Robin Léwis prepared his textbooks. I remember a time when Welsh speakers never dared; lawyers, now, standing up in court to speak Welsh, not being used to it. After those volumes came through, it''s as comfortable as any other language, and that''s been a blessing, particularly in magistrates'' courts across Wales. We have to emphasise, of course, that, as we know, every person appearing before a court has the right to use the language that they wish to use. That''s a very important principle too.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=4600&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.897131', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770588', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('bfa56c432eb2665f4061', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '9. Short Debate: Health inequalities in the south Wales coalfield', 'With a population of around 750,000, almost a quarter of Wales''s population live in the south Wales coalfield areas, covering much of the Valleys, extending from Carmarthenshire in the west to Torfaen, the eastern valley. This area has been slower to recover from the decline of the coal industry than many other former coalfield areas in Britain. The former coalfield community in my constituency extends down from Blaenavon to Pontypool, with a population of almost 25,000. Pontypool, my bro fy mebyd , and communities across Torfaen are proud places with a strong history and identity. Post-industrial communities like these have yet to fully recover from Thatcher''s de-industrialisation. Austerity has only made this worse.

 In this area, inequalities are clear to see. They''re a living scar. To highlight some of the inequalities, I''ve seen data that compares the two council areas within my constituency. These comparisons are not intended to dismiss the very real challenges faced in Monmouthshire, but to illustrate the inequalities that exist between neighbouring communities.

 Cancer incidence in the Torfaen coalfield wards is higher than the rest of my constituency. Similarly, chronic conditions are 5 per cent more common in the Torfaen wards. Mental health conditions are present in a third of GP cases in the Torfaen wards. Premature deaths and long-term illness are much higher in Torfaen than in Monmouthshire. So too are personal independence payment and universal credit claimants. This makes the residents in Torfaen much more vulnerable to UK Government benefit changes. But every statistic represents a person who may be struggling to find work, or a family who may be struggling with the health of one or more of its members, a child going to school hungry.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Matthew Jones', '', '12151', '', '', 'Plenary', '9. Short Debate: Health inequalities in the south Wales coalfield', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=15784&l=en', NULL, 22.0, 'Noise', 'archive', 'welfare_and_support', 'Housing system', '', '', 'universal credit', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:17.154599', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767147', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c017ae7f0bb1ad3478f7', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'And particularly when we''re in the realm of consolidation and accessibility.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=523&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.124361', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770346', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c03176db2af938b6ab23', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'Thank you, Chair. Going back to the interplay between wildfires and nature, it''s quite a complex interplay, isn''t it, as we discussed, with some of the things like grazing, but not overgrazing, whether we''re looking at controlled burning or firebreaks—all of that. But just to focus on the peatland restoration for a moment, because there have been some fantastic projects that have happened already. The one in the Tywi forest, for example, it''s very interesting to see the early results that are coming out from that, but also that EMBER project that''s been happening with the University of Leeds. So, could you just talk a bit about the early learnings from that and how your thoughts may or may not have changed as a result of the wildfires we''ve seen?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Vikki Howells', '', '5107', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=1679&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.507265', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771839', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c053192074c707fb11e5', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch. Well, as you''ll know, the EHRC code of practice for services, public functions and associations came into force on 5 August. We are complying with our legal duties in respect of the Equality Act 2010, and all bodies and service providers must ensure themselves that they are complying with the law. We''re absolutely clear on that. And so consistent application then requires consideration—careful consideration—of the guidance in each situation as regards those services that are being provided. And as you will know, the guidance provides examples—helpful examples—to bodies to help them apply the law in their particular circumstances. So, it''s simple: the Welsh Government expects all organisations to comply with the law, and we''ve clearly stated that, but it is for the EHRC as the regulator to be able to monitor that.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3248&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.916429', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771174', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c0d9c84b804e85d33258', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I think she''s asking which Christmas, Cabinet Minister. [ Laughter .]', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4404&l=en', NULL, 46.0, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.864041', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771852', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c0e7b29ce52ae15bc3d1', 'press_release', 'Welsh Government — Announcement', 'Welsh Government to fund interim alarm measures for leaseholders facing waking watch costs', 'Welsh Government to fund interim alarm measures for leaseholders facing waking watch costs
Welsh Government announces the creation of the Wales Interim Measures Alarm Grant. From 1 October 2026, the fund will ease the burden of expensive interim measures costs, potentially saving residents in eligible buildings hundreds of pounds each month. Some leaseholders may also be entitled to refunds if they have already paid for alarms to be installed.

Welsh Government announces the creation of the Wales Interim Measures Alarm Grant. From 1 October 2026, the fund will ease the burden of expensive interim measures costs, potentially saving residents in eligible buildings hundreds of pounds each month. Some leaseholders may also be entitled to refunds if they have already paid for alarms to be installed.

Leaseholders in Wales will be able to claim support for the cost of alarm systems, to ease the financial burden imposed by interim measures required while remediation work takes place.
From 1 October 2026, leaseholders in eligible buildings will be able to access funding through the Wales Interim Measures Alarm Grant, part of the Welsh Building Safety Fund.
The change means residents will no longer have to shoulder the cost of alarm systems needed to support a simultaneous evacuation policy while remediation work is completed.
Interim measures, such as waking watch or an upgraded alarm system, have left many leaseholders facing costs running into hundreds of pounds a month. By supporting the installation of an appropriate alarm system, this new policy will significantly reduce or remove the need for a waking watch and the financial burden it brings.
Where leaseholders have already funded the installation of interim alarms in eligible buildings an application can be made for reimbursement.
Appearing before the Senedd, the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning, Siân Gwenllian, said
:
“Since taking up this role, I have met leaseholders and listened to their stories. I have been moved by what I’ve heard. Their accounts have stayed with me, and they have shaped my determination to act.
“That is why I am pleased to announce today that I am launching a Wales Interim Measures Alarm Grant. This fund will provide financial support to leaseholders facing the costs of interim alarm systems required to support a simultaneous evacuation policy.”
Updating members on the Building Safety Remediation Programme, the Cabinet Minister said she had recently met all the developers signed up to the Welsh Government developer contract.
"I made it clear that all obligations under the contract must be met or I will not hesitate to pursue all appropriate avenues in relation to any failures. I am committed to accelerating the pace of remediation and holding developers to account."
The Cabinet Minister indicated she hopes to lay regulations under the Building Safety (Wales) Act 2026 before the Christmas recess, paving the way for additional leaseholder protections on remediation to come into force in 2027.
ENDS', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/welsh-government-to-fund-interim-alarm-measures-for-leaseholders-facing-waking-watch-costs', '2026-09-22', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Housing; Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning - Siân Gwenllian', '', '', NULL, 85.5, 'High', 'digest', 'building_safety_and_leasehold; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Property & energy', 'Housing minister', '', 'Building Safety (Wales) Act; building safety; leaseholder; leaseholders', 0, '2026-09-23T11:58:48.689089', 'newsroom:welsh-government-to-fund-interim-alarm-measures-for-leaseholders-facing-waking-watch-costs', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c160e323ddf98c2e65c7', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', 'Deputy First Minister, your Government has just announced a further £55 million for childcare, including additional funding to expand Flying Start and increase capacity. Expanding Flying Start childcare to all two-year-olds was a commitment made by Welsh Labour and Plaid Cymru under the co-operation agreement, yet that expansion is still being delivered in phases. Before asking the sector to deliver a further expansion of funded childcare, can you tell us whether the Welsh Government has identified exactly how many additional childcare places and how many additional childcare practitioners will be needed to deliver those ambitions, and, if so, will you publish those figures? Diolch yn fawr.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Helen Jenner', '', '12153', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Social Justice', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=50&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'co-operation agreement', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:15.442193', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#766795', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c1abc166ab5b05d1f695', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'The question is, ''Is that target still achievable?'' I would like to think that we can at least get closer to it, if not to it. It is going to be very challenging, in all honesty. I can''t sit here and tell you that, yes, absolutely, it will be reached. But I think we need to have that ambition and retain that ambition. I made the analogy in other contexts before. You need ambitious targets. And even if you don''t hit the target, I bet you go further than you would have had you not had that target in place. But the investments continue.

 We know the public sector very much has a big part to play. We saw some statistics this week about the benefits being brought by electric vehicle fleets within the public sector, saving millions and millions of pounds to taxpayers because of the benefits that they bring. So, we''re on that journey. I want to continue on that trajectory. And I know local government are keen to as well, because they see much of this as an invest-to-save piece as much as anything else. Whilst there very often is quite considerable capital investment upfront, you can then start realising some of those savings. So, that is something I absolutely want to do.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4773&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.925525', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771786', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c21c23a841018d50bb24', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Well—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Elfyn Llwyd', '', '12193', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1982&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.389607', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770483', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c295ea97c5988f77e8ac', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Yes, but it was held up last time, wasn''t it, Minister, because the Welsh Government wanted to input into it and ensure that it had a Welsh angle to it? That was the hold up, I was told by the previous Government, if you remember, because you were in the Chamber with me. I''m just wondering—. So, there is no hold up now. That is what you expect as a Government, and you will be adapting your policies accordingly on what you do have responsibility for in line with the law.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3332&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.928735', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771184', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c2bcb2c1589f15eb28f9', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'To be clear, we haven''t developed—. We''re in the process of developing the new strategy. I do think that there are some things in the new strategy that are valuable, but what I want to do is to refine it and to focus on the things that will truly make a difference—so that it''s not a list of the things that the Government is doing, but how they''re making a difference. I think that''s the main difference in terms of my vision. We are engaging, as I mentioned at the outset, with those groups and organisations that are experts, who have undertaken research into this, who have drawn from those international examples. 

 I would reject the suggestion, certainly about this Government—. This Government is entirely open to the best ideas, from wherever they may come. And in my meetings with the UK Government, I have proposed, for instance, in terms of the Welsh voice, that we convene the group of quadrilaterals in the UK to look at this problem holistically, to share the good practice, and discuss what could be of benefit to us, and how that interaction works in terms of our powers, but also in terms of practice and how that can be fed into what we do in the most efficient way. 

 Obviously, we do need to consider, when we look into those international examples, what is similar. You could look at what some nations do, but perhaps they have powers and a higher level of taxation, and more resources than we have. So, it has to be suitable for our powers, and for the budget that we have and the type of systems that we have in place here in Wales. But I am entirely clear that we will be drawing on good practice from wherever it may come. 

 In terms of the Cynnal payment, Scotland was the only nation in the United Kingdom, according to the recent reports that we''ve seen, where it looked like the child poverty rates were going down. They were going to increase in every other part of the UK. That is why we have adopted that, because we could see that it worked.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Sioned Williams', '', '8670', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1121&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.682471', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771213', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c2d75bab2442d250c9cb', 'press_release', 'Welsh Government — Announcement', 'Welsh Government and local government renew Strategic Partnership Agreement to strengthen trusted working relationship', 'Welsh Government and local government renew Strategic Partnership Agreement to strengthen trusted working relationship
The Welsh Government and the Welsh Local Government Association (WLGA) have renewed the Strategic Partnership Agreement, reaffirming their joint commitment to trusted partnership, fair funding and reducing bureaucracy. The renewed Agreement, launched at the WLGA Conference, reflects the priorities of the new Welsh Government while building on the strong foundations of the original Agreement.

The Welsh Government and the Welsh Local Government Association (WLGA) have renewed the Strategic Partnership Agreement, reaffirming their joint commitment to trusted partnership, fair funding and reducing bureaucracy. The renewed Agreement, launched at the WLGA Conference, reflects the priorities of the new Welsh Government while building on the strong foundations of the original Agreement.

The Welsh Government and local government have today renewed their Strategic Partnership Agreement, reaffirming a shared commitment to working together in the interests of communities across Wales.
The renewed Agreement, launched at the Welsh Local Government Association (WLGA) Conference, builds on the success of the original Agreement and reflects the commitment of both the new Welsh Government and local government to deepen and strengthen their relationship.
This renewal focuses on reasserting the underpinning principles of trusted partnership, including a joint commitment to fair funding, reducing bureaucracy and sector-led improvement.
It also includes new collective commitments relating to Welsh-speaking communities, and economic development and regeneration, alongside updated commitments on equity, inclusivity and participation, tackling health inequalities, poverty, and abuse in politics.
Both national and local government committing to the ways of working in the revised Agreement will ensure that people across Wales see the practical benefits of shared ambitions aligned with the new Welsh Government''s Programme for Government.
Siân Gwenllian MS, Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning, said: "This renewed Agreement reflects our determination, as a new Welsh Government, to build a close and trusted partnership with local government from day one.
“Councils are on the front line of delivering the services people rely on every day, and I want them to know that we will work with them, not around them, as we take forward our priorities for Wales.
“It is important that people across Wales can see the real difference this renewed relationship makes to their lives and communities."
Councillor Anthony Hunt, WLGA Leader, said: “Strong partnership between local and national government matters because the decisions we make together affect people’s everyday lives, from the care they receive and the homes they live in to their schools, jobs and local communities.
“This agreement gives us a shared foundation for working together with trust, openness and respect. By listening to councils and drawing on their understanding of the communities they serve, we can make better decisions, support services under pressure and improve outcomes for people across Wales.”', 'https://media.service.gov.wales/news/welsh-government-and-local-government-renew-strategic-partnership-agreement-to-strengthen-trusted-working-relationship', '2026-09-17', '', '', '', '', '', 'Welsh Government', 'Local Government; Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning - Siân Gwenllian', '', '', NULL, 54.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Sector bodies; Housing minister', '', 'programme for government', 0, '2026-09-18T11:37:46.079599', 'newsroom:welsh-government-and-local-government-renew-strategic-partnership-agreement-to-strengthen-trusted-working-relationship', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c316f17fe0865c0b165c', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch. We''ve been engaging for a little while now at official level on this. The Deputy First Minister met with Baroness Sherlock, who is a Minister in the Lords, recently, to start that political engagement. We''re making the case around Cynnal being disregarded. It''s a clear part of us sharing the ambition around tackling poverty. We believe it''s the right way forward, so we are doing that at pace with them and they recognise our desire to do that at pace.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Claire Germain', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1027&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.667793', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771194', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c32d86327df9383dbbfa', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', 'Thank you very much for raising an issue of real importance, something I know that many in this Senedd are concerned about and are passionate about. Certainly, there was a clear commitment in the Plaid Cymru manifesto—and that strong commitment remains within Plaid Cymru—to ensure that the community right to buy does become law. I''m not going to be attempting to make any statements before we hear from the First Minister later this afternoon, but, certainly, I hope that that unambiguous commitment on the importance of that has been made clear by me this afternoon.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16085', '2026-07-14', 'Heledd Fychan', '', '426', '', '', 'Plenary', '3. Business Statement and Announcement', '16085', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16085?startPos=7687&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'social_housing', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'community right to buy; right to buy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:08.889087', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16085&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767737', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c3324b331000dbbff0be', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', 'Diolch, Ddirprwy Lywydd. Minister, it''s difficult—it is always difficult—to follow Gwyn. In some ways, he''s always been my warm-up act, but I''ve always been a flop. [ Laughter .] Some of the people that spoke today have been absolutely fantastic with their lived experiences, and it shows the depth of the problems that exist. In a very minor, minor way, I ended up having to go to England to have an operation about eight years ago, because my eyesight got so bad for a number of reasons. I had new lenses put in and I just couldn''t work. It reached the point that I couldn''t work. So, these things really do matter, and they improve life dramatically.

 A few things about Gwyn. Gwyn, obviously, knows exactly what he''s talking about, the same as Safa, and what possibly people also know is he''s really passionate about what he does. However, as an ophthalmologist, he is one of the top people that you would ever get. He travels internationally to show how to do things, and he does also go abroad to help wherever he can. So, in that respect, what he''s pushing here really, really does need support, and I''m glad everybody so far has come this way.

 I''m going to be a bit of a damp squib. Everybody''s talked so well, and, just like James said, there''s no real point in pushing those things over and over and over again. What I''m going to do is bring it down to brass tacks. The whole thing, apart from being absolutely fantastically wonderful to do it, it is essentially something that''s going to save the NHS money, it''s going to produce huge amounts more productivity, and, as a lovely by-product, it''s going to affect so many people''s lives.

 Plaid Cymru have a fantastic part in our manifesto, which is essentially the hubs—the place where specialists can stand alone beyond all the battle that''s going on in the NHS, where they can focus, and they can rely on predictable methods, because people are coming in, everything''s controlled, which lead to the productivity gains. These are the simple things that really change. What you do is you get more people through, you get more pleasant working, because it isn''t that stress that you''ve got in accident and emergency, and people can focus on the specialisms, and therefore everything increases in terms of the ability to do what you can do.

 Substantial productivity gains offered by surgical hubs, which is what we''re proposing, Minister, is something I''ve experienced quite recently. My wife had an operation, and the ability to move through the process is so slick. It''s like a production line; that''s the only way I can describe it. So, it actually does work. Implementing this across Wales is, without doubt, the solution to the problems that we have. Surgical hubs also mean there''s less outsourcing; we''re saving money. I had to go private when I went to England to get it done, but other people will have to—. Previously, Labour have been paying quite heavily for treatment to be outsourced and for people to have operations privately but paid for by the NHS, which isn''t anywhere near a long-term solution. Staff retention in the NHS and in this particular area as well is massively difficult, and the very fact that you''ve got good working conditions, you''ve got high productivity and happy people means that you''re going to have better productivity just as a result and people will want to stay. Safa mentioned in a talk a few days ago that, of a cohort of seven ophthalmologist doctors coming into, I assume, Singleton Hospital, within a very short time, four of them had left, just because the systems were dysfunctional, they weren''t happy. And it really does make a difference.

 This doesn''t just affect health, but also can affect what we do outside. You''ve got people who are far more productive. With me, I could not work, I couldn''t drive, and from knowing that I couldn''t even see colour, all of a sudden, being able to see colour and a clock on the wall was mad—totally mad. But these things do change people''s ability to be productive so, so much and it had a huge impact on me.

 The brain drain that we''ve got because these doctors are leaving here, we just cannot allow to happen. In my opinion, the hubs that we have and the ways and the systems that we have to work will facilitate people to be happier and to stay.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'John Davies', '', '4650', '', '', 'Plenary', '6. Member Debate under Standing Order 11.21(iv): Ophthalmology services', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=10562&l=en', NULL, 38.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'hmos_and_standards', 'Private rented sector', '', '', 'damp', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.558046', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767045', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c347241482b059990d78', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'I think that there are two comments that I would make to begin with. In the first instance, I think that, at times—and I tread carefully here as someone who was not a Member of previous Seneddau—there has been a tendency to make declaratory legislation, which says, ''We will be the best in the world at this, that or the other'', and not necessarily to follow that up in detail, and therefore I think that there needs to be a close focus on the policy intent of legislation. And it goes to the comments made earlier by the Member for Caerdydd Penarth around the financing and such issues. I think that there needs to be that focus, and that does probably mean less declaratory legislation. 

 I''ll give you one example where the Government is actively considering whether legislation is needed or not, which is the new development agency. There are mechanisms to establish the development agency on a non-legislative basis; there are mechanisms that would require legislation. That is an active conversation, and it''s an example of one of those things where we''ve said that we would consider whether we do need legislation and we are actively looking and working through those issues with the expert panel, with business groups, to consider whether that is necessary or not. So, it''s a healthy scepticism, I would suggest.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=5034&l=en', NULL, 92.0, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Legislation Committee; expert panel', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:10', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770511', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c356ed3e558438bdaca6', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch, Chair. Minister, just firstly, I''d like to ask—. You obviously inherited a lot of action plans—equality action plans—from the last Government. I was just wondering which ones you were going to adopt.

 Secondly, I think we took big strides in terms of equality in the last Senedd. And I don''t know about you, Minister, and Jayne, but I was very proud to be a part of that. And that was, of course, the British Sign Language (Wales) Act 2026. I thought that was a wonderful thing that we managed to achieve in the last Senedd—I''m sorry, and Jane over there as well. I''m just wondering where you are with the implementation of that. Diolch.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=4721&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.041054', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771289', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c3a1a7b5daca14ebb412', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Bore da. Claire Germain, deputy director of tackling poverty.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Claire Germain', '', '', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=102&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.566109', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771145', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c3bb50e7c6c6472a5a8b', 'oral_question', 'Question not reached in Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '<p>We committed in our manifesto to exploring opportunities for further permitted development rights to allow community-led housing and make it easier to convert empty buildings on high streets and spaces above shops into residences. This will be achieved through changes to regulations.</p>', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', '', 'Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '', '', '', 'Plenary', 'Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Local Government, Housing and Planning', '16086', '', NULL, 55.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'planning_system', 'Planning & place', 'Housing minister', '', 'permitted development', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:07.005933', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=QNR', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c420d2e788ba0c1dd6f8', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'One of the things we''ve been discussing is how you effectively train policy development colleagues to be aware of the legislative context and how to engage most effectively with that. Much of this work is ongoing. I''m not suggesting that we are anywhere close to having resolved these issues, but it is one of those areas that is very much on our agenda and we''re working towards it.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Dafydd Trystan Davies', '', '12140', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=3104&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.618379', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770479', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c430990bb1beae3f82d4', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Thank you, Chair. This is probably a question for the Cabinet Minister. The First Minister has outlined his legislative programme for the next 18 months. The Government is a minority Government, of course. We''ve heard a great deal already this morning about capacity—capacity within the team, but also capacity in terms of time in the Senedd. Do you acknowledge the risk of spending a great deal of time and expending effort on legislation that will, ultimately, be at risk of not passing in the Senedd because of the electoral arithmetic? What will the Government be doing to ensure that that is avoided?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=1531&l=en', NULL, 74.8, 'Medium', 'digest', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context; committee_scrutiny', 'Context; Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee; legislative programme', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.305876', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770398', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4a15881b2bb95948f0b', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Absolutely, so—. Sorry, Chair. So, this Government now has issued that guidance to all your relevant bodies, and your Ministers are adapting policies and the LGBTQ+ action plan, for example, to adhere to that, or is there more of a timeline, that we''re still reviewing?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=3309&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.923143', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771179', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4b8745931edb4e59c5c', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'It''s the same. Whilst we are very lucky in Powys, as we have good relationships with our department—I have an excellent data protection officer who backs up the EROs'' power to request data—having regular data sets updated is a problem. That will be the case across lots of local authority areas, just because the teams themselves that maintain those records, they have to supply them, and that relationship needs to be built up over time, not just ad hoc.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Sandra Matthews', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=5381&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.413304', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769993', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4e973ec649c415807dd', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', 'I welcome this timely question from the Member, on the same day my Government publishes plans to cut business rates by 30 per cent for pubs, restaurants, cafes, bars, licensed clubs, hotels, gyms and cinemas. More broadly, through Business Wales, we provide advice and growth support to businesses. Targeted investment in local infrastructure and funding is helping to revitalise Bangor city centre specifically. We have also established that town-centre taskforce we promised we would as part of our 100-day commitment to drive investment, increase footfall and support local businesses to grow.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16260', '2026-09-15', 'Rhun ap Iorwerth', 'First Minister of Wales', '2717', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the First Minister', '16260', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16260?startPos=439&l=en', NULL, 60.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property', 'Tax & finance', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'business rates', 0, '2026-09-19T12:39:24.889933', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16260&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768977', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4ef95fc67e80f9b3d2f', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', 'I think we can all agree that Wales is very much a world-class destination for tourism. That moment when you see someone posing for a photograph outside a castle or looking around at many of our museums or enjoying our national park—that is a special moment, and it should make us all proud. Because, of course, Wales does actually belong to all of us, and we''re very lucky that we can share our beautiful country with people from all over the world. I often enjoy having family over and taking them up to Pen y Fan and getting them to do that early morning walk up the mountain, and, of course, the many lush waterfalls that we have.

 But getting back to our capital city for a moment, we''ve had some incredible success lately in putting Cardiff on the map. In just the last decade, we hosted the UEFA Champions League final, which smashed the previous footfall record by bringing more than 310,000 people into our great city. We hosted Clash at the Castle, a real global event, and the company''s first stadium event in the UK for over 30 years. Most of these spectators came from outside Wales, and when they were surveyed, they said they just wanted to explore a bit more. Last year, we had the European Rugby Champions Cup, and with the eyes of the world on them, Oasis chose Cardiff to kick off their reunion tour. All of these major events bring thousands and thousands of people who want to spend their time and money in Wales, and this brings me to the topic of this debate.

 The Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy) Etc. (Wales) Act 2025, which only became law last year, isn''t even coming into effect anywhere until next April. So, we haven''t had a chance yet to assess the positive impact that it can have on our communities. To be clear, this gave councils the choice to introduce a small charge on overnight accommodation after they had consulted with local residents and businesses. In Cardiff, we have chosen to introduce the levy, and it will come into effect next April. Anglesey, Conwy and Gwynedd councils are currently consulting, and they will make their decisions later this year. The challenges and the opportunities around the visitor economy will ebb and flow, and all councils are free to make their decisions in the future. And that''s exactly the point here: this is a great example of devolving power beyond the Senedd, taking decisions closer to the people who live there.

 Reform would have you believe that this is a tourism tax—they call it so, and they say that it''s going to cost people in the future. On this idea that it is going to deter people from coming to Wales and spending their money here, the truth is that this is a visitor levy where local councils choose to adopt it and decide. It''s £1.30 for a night. You can''t even get a pint or a coffee for that much money. That''s what we''re talking about here—a very small contribution from people who have chosen to visit Wales, which will collectively make a real big difference.

 Councils who adopt the visitor levy will spend that money on improving their tourism offer, protecting local nature and landmarks, and promoting Welsh culture. Surely these are objectives that we all support. Most importantly, the people who choose to come to Wales will be happy to support them too. This is a way of making sure that our visitor economy grows and the benefits feed back into our communities, creating good jobs, revitalising our towns and cities and protecting Welsh culture for future generations to enjoy.

 This is all about making sure that the infrastructure works as well. This Government''s amendment makes reference to two specific policies—', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16084', '2026-07-08', 'Shav Taj', '', '12175', '', '', 'Plenary', '7. Reform UK Debate: The tourism sector', '16084', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16084?startPos=12736&l=en', NULL, 110.0, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; second_homes_and_short_term_lets; named_welsh_legislation', 'Context; Planning & place; Tax & finance', '', '', 'Visitor Accommodation (Register and Levy); tourism tax; visitor accommodation; visitor levy', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:16.834114', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16084&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#767081', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c4f1e233648b3455f74b', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', 'Everyone''s looking at me, so I guess I''m going to begin. As a pilot authority, ours is quite a lived experience. They were successful in Powys. It was possible to use local data sets to identify individuals that weren''t registered and send them a notice of registration. Again, those notices of registration were generally quite well received. We had a very low number of opt-outs. Obviously, where people did feel strongly and did not wish to be automatically registered, they felt very strongly, as I''m sure you''ve all experienced on the doorstep sometimes. Overall, as a process, it was successful. We found just over 5,000 individuals, which worked out at approximately 5 per cent of our register. I think there was overall confidence that the people we found were accurate. They had a link to the property. Having the assurance of finding an individual on two or three or even four data sets gave us that assurance that there was quality and accuracy in that result.

 I''m sure, from the elector''s perspective, it felt very automatic and very seamless. From the administrator''s experience in the back office, it was probably anything but. There was a lot of manual intervention and work, which I think the Electoral Commission report does highlight. Data matching relies on good-quality and standardised data, none of which is what we have in local authorities. A lot of the data sets have been created for other purposes. They''re maintained not because that person is resident at the property, but maybe for another reason, et cetera. So, it is difficult to evaluate certain data sets and their usefulness until we actually receive them. In fact, there were two or three data sets that we received that didn''t have addresses on, or the quality and completeness of their addresses weren''t good enough for us to even use. So, it was balancing that expectation of what we requested and what we thought we were going to get with what actually arrived. 

 Data cleansing the data sets that were of use was quite time consuming, stripping out the incomplete records, ensuring that they were all of a standard good enough to take forward. And even within those data sets, obviously you have inconsistencies that an individual might use with regard to their name or even an address, and I''ll give an example of that. On one data set, somebody might be known as David John Edwards. That individual might also be known as Dai John Edwards. On another data set, it might be D.J. Edwards, or just good old Dave. Trying to match all of those up and make the assumption that that''s one and the same can be quite difficult. And it''s obviously not 100 per cent then unless you''ve got some other information such as a date of birth to link them. To add to that complexity, then we add children into the mix. Something I always say is that parents can sometimes lack imagination when they name their children and we might have another D.J. Edwards at the property. If we''re lucky, it might be a David Jasper, for example—same initial, but at least there''s a distinction of a middle name. If we''re very unlucky, the worst-case scenario is we have a David John Jr. Again, there are quite a few data sets that don''t have ''Sr.'' or ''Jr.'' So unless we have a date of birth to identify the difference—. All I can say is opening the post in those addresses must be quite fun. And this happens more often than we realise.

 Those sorts of issues meant that there was a lot of manual checking and, even after the data match exercise, human eye-balling the records that came out before we then actually wrote out. And it''s not just elector information; property addresses come in all sorts of incarnations. There is a standard for an address. There''s a unique property reference number, which some of you may be aware of. It''s called a UPRN. However, again, in Powys, our council tax records don''t have a fully complete UPRN on every address. I know that some local authorities across Wales do have that, because when they data match during the canvas, that makes their life a lot easier. I think there''s about 50-odd per cent of our council tax that have UPRNs. So, that was the level of accuracy: we know that, if that number and that number matches, it''s exact. However, for the other proportion, that''s not so possible. And there are a lot of the data sets that don''t have UPRNs, so any discrepancies in the way addresses have been typed up—. For example, you could have ''1 High Street'', you could have ''Flat 1 High Street'', ''Flat 1'' without a property name and ''High Street''—are all of those incarnations the same? If you didn''t have a UPRN number or maybe a surname that linked them, that sometimes, as well, could be quite difficult, and we did find incomplete addresses being a problem.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Sandra Matthews', '', '', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '3. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 2', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=3747&l=en', NULL, 109.2, 'High', 'digest', 'taxation_of_property; committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system; Tax & finance', '', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; council tax; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:49.159479', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769913', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c5490e10863f2dda3b01', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Just one question from me, to finish off, and it''s probably a ''yes'' or a ''no'', so it might make it easier for you. Are you planning on bringing forward any consolidation Bills within this Senedd term?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Peredur Owen Griffiths', '', '8579', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=5265&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:10.060895', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770551', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c56b7b720b2606b28af0', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Diolch, Chair. In regard to community cohesion, I was just wondering about—. I''m interested in the cost of those programmes and how you''re measuring success. But also, I think that—and I''ve banged on an awful lot since being a politician—sport is often very underestimated in terms of, as well as the physical, the mental health benefits of it. It''s an enormous leveller and enabler in terms of ensuring community cohesion, actually, and it''s something that we don''t perhaps use enough. So, how are you working—my question is to you—cross-Government to ensure that you re-emphasise the importance of community cohesion and use what we''ve already got in place to help with that?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Laura Anne Jones', '', '4562', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=6550&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.242567', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771312', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c57910493a525c337242', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'The difficulty we''ve had, of course, is that it''s been a bit of a spinning door in terms of Prime Ministers, Secretaries of State and Ministers of State as well. So, we are making it clear continually our expectation of UK Government, particularly around some of these areas. On energy, we will make the case for further devolution so that we can have more responsibility over those things that we feel are important to us. But those are ongoing and continual discussions. The UK Government have ambitions. Whether they match ours remains to be seen, but certainly—. I don''t know, on an official''s level, Rhodri, is there anything that you want to add?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Llyr Gruffydd', '', '425', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=4428&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.870819', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771901', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c58cd625da4ac0071797', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you, Chair.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=776&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.639288', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771207', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c5e0bb62632a08a6a9a2', 'plenary_transcript', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', 'I want to see more money in our NHS. You could put more money into the NHS if you listened to us and stopped funding some of your vanity projects like planting trees abroad, like some of the projects that you''re doing close to home, wasting Welsh Government money looking at Welsh independence, something the public overwhelmingly did not vote for. That''s the money that could go into the NHS. We''ve also heard about ''small change'' for overseas offices. That''s money that could go into our NHS. That is the reason why we voted against your supplementary budget, because it''s a bad deal for Wales.

 You just waffled then in your answer to me, Minister, because you didn''t actually say when we were going to have those two-year waits gone. You''ve said ''months''. You''ve mentioned elective care hubs. By the time they go through planning, by the time the Welsh Government shuffles paper around their offices, that''s going to end up being years. You know how the system works. You sat on committee with me when we discussed these things. They don''t come overnight. So, can you now outline a date when two-year waits will be eliminated in Wales, because far too many people have been waiting in pain? You promised people you would sort this out, and it''s about time you started to deliver, because your honeymoon period cannot go on forever.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Plenary/16086', '2026-07-15', 'James Evans', '', '8585', '', '', 'Plenary', '1. Questions to the Cabinet Minister for Health and Care', '16086', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16086?startPos=824&l=en', NULL, 27.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'fiscal_and_legislative_context', 'Context', 'Other committee chairs', '', 'supplementary budget', 0, '2026-08-04T14:03:04.750725', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16086&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#768059', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c5e37047516f05ae649c', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'There''s so much that you could put into this strategy—it''s so important. But I just want some kind of assurance from you, if that''s all right, that the strategy will include people who are refugees, women who have disabilities, people who are seeking asylum, for instance—that''s one thing. Women who go into prison—we do know that, usually, they have had experiences of violence, unfortunately.

 And finally, I just wanted to follow up as well—. We do know, don''t we, how important it is to prevent this, and that is working with boys and young men. Could you give us some assurance about the first two things, and then talk a little about the work that is going on, or is being provided, for boys and young men via education? Thank you very much.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Jane Dodds', '', '4983', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=5588&l=en', NULL, 86.2, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Opposition housing leads', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:36.137771', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771255', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c66ddd6ec7d3abd9baac', 'committee_transcript', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', 'Thank you very much.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16254', '2026-09-21', 'Huw Thomas', '', '12156', '', '', 'Legislation Committee', '3. General Scrutiny session with the Counsel General and the Cabinet Minister for Government Effectiveness and the Constitution', '16254', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16254?startPos=2354&l=en', NULL, 57.5, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet', '', 'Legislation Committee', 0, '2026-09-22T11:55:09.477568', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16254&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#770444', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c6a137ff257eee9dc7b6', 'committee_transcript', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', 'I was going to come in on a similar point to Niamh about the impact this is going to have now over the coming months, immediately, for farmers with these things like feed stocks—I won''t go over that—but also the knock-on effects, I suppose, to other parts of Government, with food prices. The extreme heat, fires et cetera—it isn''t just in Wales, it''s across Europe, with the wider supply chain. So, to what extent is the Government, or you, having discussions with colleagues about what implications this will have on our wider economy and on the cost of living?', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16233', '2026-09-23', 'Anna Nicholl', '', '12178', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', '2. General scrutiny of the Cabinet Minister for Rural Resilience and Sustainability', '16233', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16233?startPos=2373&l=en', NULL, 34.5, 'Low', 'dashboard', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', '', '', 'Climate Change, Environment, Sustainability and Rural Affairs Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:34.638440', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16233&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771937', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c6a1c6dcf7c5f8c78242', 'committee_transcript', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', 'Thank you. Laura, and then we''ll move on to the next area.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16247', '2026-09-23', 'Zaynub Akbar', '', '12141', '', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', '2. General scrutiny session: Deputy First Minister and Cabinet Minister for Equality and Social Justice', '16247', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16247?startPos=1051&l=en', NULL, 69.0, 'Medium', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'First Minister / Cabinet; Other committee chairs', '', 'Equality, Human Rights and Social Justice Committee', 0, '2026-09-25T06:30:35.672216', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16247&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#771202', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO items (uid, source_kind, source_name, title, body, url, item_date, speaker, speaker_role, speaker_id, party, constituency, forum, agenda_item, meeting_id, video_url, deadline, score, band, channel, themes, tiers, entities, signals, matched_terms, force_alert, collected_at, raw_ref, notified_at, reviewed_by, review_note, review_status) VALUES ('c6e3ae49e5e406e715c1', 'committee_transcript', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', 'Thank you. Thank you, Chair.', 'https://record.senedd.wales/Committee/16256', '2026-09-17', 'Peter Fox', '', '4787', '', '', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee', '2. The Representation of the People (Electoral Registration without Applications) (Electoral Reform) (Wales) Regulations 2026: Evidence session 1', '16256', 'http://www.senedd.tv/en/16256?startPos=1285&l=en', NULL, 97.7, 'High', 'digest', 'committee_scrutiny', 'Housing system', 'Opposition housing leads', 'Opportunity to influence', 'Local Government, Housing and Planning Committee; evidence session', 0, '2026-09-18T11:33:48.877707', 'https://record.senedd.wales/XMLExport/Download?meetingID=16256&xmlDownloadType=EnglishTranscript#769861', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
